9, 20102, 1212, 264, 72657, 13, 151645], "pred": "By following these detailed steps, you can prepare a blood slide suitable for viewing under a microscope.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.511420568146329, "g_worst_claim": -0.059870004653930664, "g_content": 0.6726005664770154, "g_traj_mean": 0.47462207078933716}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 10549, 264, 6543, 14983, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 10771, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 624, 17, 13, 2691, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 624, 18, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 323, 3331, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 624, 19, 13, 19338, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 773, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 624, 20, 13, 22950, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 21, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 624, 22, 13, 9646, 31620, 11, 1992, 1105, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees.\n4. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n5. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n6. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.\n7. Once dried, place them in the provided slide transport containers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.9185965230813613, "g_worst_claim": 0.40123462677001953, "g_content": 2.1400491465692935, "g_traj_mean": 1.5345791578292847}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 10549, 264, 6543, 14983, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 10771, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 323, 912, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 624, 17, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 11, 3331, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 11, 323, 5796, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 773, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 624, 18, 13, 22950, 279, 14983, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 19, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 624, 20, 13, 9646, 31620, 11, 1992, 279, 21521, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 13, 151645], "pred": "To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end.\n2. Take another clean slide, hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees, and touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n3. Push the slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n4. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.\n5. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.875195443243628, "g_worst_claim": 0.40123462677001953, "g_content": 2.04273021922392, "g_traj_mean": 1.5491838455200195}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.78, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "90", "question": "how to prepare blood slide", "gt": "To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\n\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so that it runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\n4. Push the second slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n5. Repeat step 4 to make two smears.\n6. Allow the smears to air dry.\n7. Label the slides clearly.\n8. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.\n\nNote: It is important to sterilize the needle used to obtain the blood sample by heating it with a flame before using it. Additionally, it is essential to wear rubber gloves when handling blood to avoid coming into contact with potentially infectious materials.", "pred": "These steps ensure that the blood is spread evenly and thinly on the slide, which is essential for viewing and analysis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "These steps ensure that the blood is spread evenly and thinly on the slide, which is essential for viewing and analysis.", "cot": "[\"To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees.\\n4. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\\n5. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\\n6. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.\\n7. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.\\n\\nThese steps ensure that the blood is spread evenly and thinly on the slide, which is essential for viewing and analysis.\", \"To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\\n4. Make 2 smears, allowing them to air dry.\\n5. Label the slides clearly once they are dried.\\n6. Place the dried slides in the provided slide transport containers.\", \"To prepare a blood slide, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface and add one small drop of blood to one end.\\n2. Take another clean slide, hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees, and touch the blood with one end of the slide. Allow the blood to run along the edge of the slide by capillary action.\\n3. Push the slide carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\\n4. Make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly.\\n5. Once dried, place the slides in the provided slide transport containers.\\n\\nSpecifically, the process involves using a clean glass slide and a second slide held at a 45-degree angle to create a thin layer of blood. The blood is spread along the length of the first slide, and then both slides are allowed to dry before being labeled and stored.\", \"Prepare a clean glass slide, place a small drop of blood at one end, take another clean slide, and holding it at an angle of about 45 degrees, touch the blood with one end of the slide to allow the blood to run along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Then carefully push the slide along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. It is recommended to make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in the provided slide transport containers.\"]", "time_sec": 24.112, "generation_time_seconds": 23.4904, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6149, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 10549, 264, 6543, 14983, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 10771, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 624, 17, 13, 2691, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 624, 18, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 323, 3331, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 624, 19, 13, 19338, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 773, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 624, 20, 13, 22950, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 21, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 624, 22, 13, 9646, 31620, 11, 1992, 279, 21521, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 382, 9485, 7354, 5978, 429, 279, 6543, 374, 8865, 41047, 323, 98431, 389, 279, 14983, 11, 892, 374, 7565, 369, 20102, 323, 6358, 13, 151645], "pred": "These steps ensure that the blood is spread evenly and thinly on the slide, which is essential for viewing and analysis.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6510917137033299, "g_worst_claim": 0.22886300086975098, "g_content": 1.9143245697021485, "g_traj_mean": 1.3487093448638916}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 10549, 264, 6543, 14983, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 10771, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 624, 17, 13, 2691, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 624, 18, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 323, 3331, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 13, 19338, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 773, 429, 279, 6543, 8473, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 13, 22950, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 19, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 10693, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 624, 20, 13, 9402, 279, 21521, 9355, 3055, 807, 525, 31620, 624, 21, 13, 10771, 279, 31620, 21521, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. Place a clean glass slide on a flat surface.\n2. Add one small drop of blood to one end of the slide.\n3. Take another clean slide and hold it at an angle of about 45 degrees. Touch the blood with one end of the slide so that the blood runs along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Push carefully along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood.\n4. Make 2 smears, allowing them to air dry.\n5. Label the slides clearly once they are dried.\n6. Place the dried slides in the provided slide transport containers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.818326820264481, "g_worst_claim": 0.3693932294845581, "g_content": 2.107489787356954, "g_traj_mean": 1.5628705024719238}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 10549, 264, 6543, 14983, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 10771, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 389, 264, 10063, 7329, 323, 912, 825, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 311, 825, 835, 624, 17, 13, 11778, 2441, 4240, 14983, 11, 3331, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 11, 323, 5796, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 13, 26530, 279, 6543, 311, 1598, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 624, 18, 13, 22950, 279, 14983, 15516, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 624, 19, 13, 7405, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 624, 20, 13, 9646, 31620, 11, 1992, 279, 21521, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 382, 47514, 745, 11, 279, 1882, 17601, 1667, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 323, 264, 2086, 14983, 5644, 518, 264, 220, 19, 20, 37675, 9210, 311, 1855, 264, 15430, 6193, 315, 6543, 13, 576, 6543, 374, 8865, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 11, 323, 1221, 2176, 21521, 525, 5420, 311, 9058, 1573, 1660, 29829, 323, 9768, 13, 151645], "pred": "Specifically, the process involves using a clean glass slide and a second slide held at a 45-degree angle to create a thin layer of blood. The blood is spread along the length of the first slide, and then both slides are allowed to dry before being labeled and stored.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4628555662827063, "g_worst_claim": 0.08956290284792583, "g_content": 1.6293565432230632, "g_traj_mean": 1.1083621978759766}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [50590, 264, 4240, 8991, 14983, 11, 1992, 264, 2613, 5943, 315, 6543, 518, 825, 835, 11, 1896, 2441, 4240, 14983, 11, 323, 9963, 432, 518, 458, 9210, 315, 911, 220, 19, 20, 12348, 11, 5796, 279, 6543, 448, 825, 835, 315, 279, 14983, 311, 2138, 279, 6543, 311, 1598, 3156, 279, 6821, 315, 279, 14983, 553, 2062, 34505, 1917, 13, 5005, 15516, 4484, 279, 14983, 3156, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 1156, 14983, 311, 8193, 264, 15430, 80503, 315, 6543, 13, 1084, 374, 11102, 311, 1281, 220, 17, 1525, 7444, 11, 2138, 1105, 311, 3720, 9058, 11, 323, 2383, 1105, 9355, 1573, 24047, 1105, 304, 279, 3897, 14983, 7557, 23853, 13, 151645], "pred": "Prepare a clean glass slide, place a small drop of blood at one end, take another clean slide, and holding it at an angle of about 45 degrees, touch the blood with one end of the slide to allow the blood to run along the edge of the slide by capillary action. Then carefully push the slide along the length of the first slide to produce a thin smear of blood. It is recommended to make 2 smears, allow them to air dry, and label them clearly before placing them in the provided slide transport containers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6469429524390253, "g_worst_claim": 0.5682582378387451, "g_content": 2.4011158336292615, "g_traj_mean": 1.517176866531372}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.9, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "91", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are ones that the body cannot produce on its own, thus they must be obtained through diet. In adults, there are eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. For infants, histidine is also considered essential. On the other hand, nonessential amino acids are those that the body can produce, even if they are not present in the diet. These include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. While not essential to obtain from diet, these amino acids are still necessary as they are used by the body to build proteins needed for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.", "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids are those that can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down other proteins.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids are those that can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down other proteins.", "cot": "[\"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or protein breakdown products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan for adults, plus histidine for infants. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids are those that can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down other proteins.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet. Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet.\"]", "time_sec": 14.814, "generation_time_seconds": 14.1603, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6455, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7504668545413327, "g_worst_claim": 0.6825928006853376, "g_content": 1.3235732078552247, "g_traj_mean": 0.731877863407135}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 279, 3738, 2487, 4157, 8193, 389, 1181, 1828, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 12833, 29985, 3871, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 369, 12598, 11, 5519, 12744, 89973, 369, 41434, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or protein breakdown products. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan for adults, plus histidine for infants. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5503976250016118, "g_worst_claim": 0.013292953372001648, "g_content": 0.8275362650553385, "g_traj_mean": 0.4349703788757324}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 773, 807, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 2987, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 1008, 27796, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids are those that can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down other proteins.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6219732026968683, "g_worst_claim": -0.006441716636930194, "g_content": 1.383369627453032, "g_traj_mean": 0.6261135339736938}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 773, 498, 1969, 633, 1105, 504, 697, 9968, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 1393, 7565, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 773, 498, 1969, 633, 1105, 504, 697, 9968, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet. Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7592746812190736, "g_worst_claim": 0.22254566712812943, "g_content": 0.7557756250554865, "g_traj_mean": 0.8314115405082703}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.53, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "92", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "cot": "[\"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot synthesize and must be obtained from dietary sources, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body through the breakdown of proteins or through the conversion of other amino acids. Essential amino acids are crucial for the proper functioning of the body and the synthesis of proteins, while nonessential amino acids are not required for the body's basic functions and can be obtained through the breakdown of proteins or other amino acids.\", \"Essential amino acids are necessary for the body's functions and cannot be produced by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down proteins, so they are not essential to obtain from the diet.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce and must be obtained through diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\"]", "time_sec": 12.891, "generation_time_seconds": 12.1897, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6943, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7422588707564713, "g_worst_claim": 0.6841009684971401, "g_content": 1.3150731086730958, "g_traj_mean": 0.719383180141449}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 279, 3738, 2487, 4157, 6782, 26887, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 33525, 8173, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 1526, 279, 29985, 315, 27796, 476, 1526, 279, 14409, 315, 1008, 41400, 32869, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 525, 16587, 369, 279, 6169, 30201, 315, 279, 2487, 323, 279, 38875, 315, 27796, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 537, 2567, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 6770, 5746, 323, 646, 387, 12180, 1526, 279, 29985, 315, 27796, 476, 1008, 41400, 32869, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot synthesize and must be obtained from dietary sources, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body through the breakdown of proteins or through the conversion of other amino acids. Essential amino acids are crucial for the proper functioning of the body and the synthesis of proteins, while nonessential amino acids are not required for the body's basic functions and can be obtained through the breakdown of proteins or other amino acids.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.17302858311978597, "g_worst_claim": -0.08421791262096828, "g_content": 0.2684776173081509, "g_traj_mean": 0.174604594707489}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 5871, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 5746, 323, 4157, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 773, 807, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 27796, 11, 773, 807, 525, 537, 7565, 311, 6851, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are necessary for the body's functions and cannot be produced by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down proteins, so they are not essential to obtain from the diet.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.4076984725931361, "g_worst_claim": 0.15183972120285033, "g_content": 0.6516949580265925, "g_traj_mean": 0.44033464789390564}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 279, 2487, 4157, 8193, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce and must be obtained through diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6590395142333676, "g_worst_claim": 0.4723596043056912, "g_content": 1.2615971816213507, "g_traj_mean": 0.6533775925636292}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "94", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between essential and non-essential amino acids are:\n\nEssential Amino Acids:\n\n* Must be obtained from the diet\n* Cannot be produced by the body\n* Nine out of 20 amino acids are considered essential (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine for infants)\n\nNon-Essential Amino Acids:\n\n* Can be produced by the body\n* Not necessarily required to obtain from the diet\n* Include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine\n\nIt is important to note that even though non-essential amino acids can be produced by the body, it is still important to consume a balanced diet that includes all of the essential amino acids to ensure proper bodily functions.", "pred": "Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This means that while they are not essential for human survival, they contribute to the overall protein structure and function in the body. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This means that while they are not essential for human survival, they contribute to the overall protein structure and function in the body. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "cot": "[\"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\", \"The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids lies in their necessity for human consumption. Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be obtained through diet. These essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, as well as histidine which is essential for infants.\\n\\nNonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This means that while they are not essential for human survival, they contribute to the overall protein structure and function in the body. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids lies in their availability to the human body. Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained from the diet. These include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan for adults, as well as histidine for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine, can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products. These nonessential amino acids are not essential for obtaining from the diet.\"]", "time_sec": 19.126, "generation_time_seconds": 18.2038, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9131, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7512442683760738, "g_worst_claim": 0.7027027493431455, "g_content": 1.3287710189819335, "g_traj_mean": 0.7303348779678345}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5812078582889894, "g_worst_claim": 0.08993175625801086, "g_content": 1.0507061640421549, "g_traj_mean": 0.6368252038955688}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 15448, 304, 862, 31861, 369, 3738, 15293, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 3738, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 9968, 13, 4220, 7565, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 438, 1632, 438, 12744, 89973, 892, 374, 7565, 369, 41434, 382, 8121, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 1096, 3363, 429, 1393, 807, 525, 537, 7565, 369, 3738, 19661, 11, 807, 16792, 311, 279, 8084, 12833, 5944, 323, 729, 304, 279, 2487, 13, 25311, 315, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. This means that while they are not essential for human survival, they contribute to the overall protein structure and function in the body. Examples of nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.4311223201593033, "g_worst_claim": -0.0021839037537574766, "g_content": 0.7408847024995987, "g_traj_mean": 0.43822017312049866}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 15448, 304, 862, 18048, 311, 279, 3738, 2487, 13, 47733, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 279, 2487, 4157, 8193, 389, 1181, 1828, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 4220, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 369, 12598, 11, 438, 1632, 438, 12744, 89973, 369, 41434, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 1741, 438, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 11, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 4220, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 525, 537, 7565, 369, 19009, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 151645], "pred": "The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids lies in their availability to the human body. Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained from the diet. These include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan for adults, as well as histidine for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, such as alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine, can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products. These nonessential amino acids are not essential for obtaining from the diet.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.59474751002571, "g_worst_claim": 0.0036192763419378372, "g_content": 0.8452996722722458, "g_traj_mean": 0.4635821282863617}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.39, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "95", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nEssential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nPassage 1 states that nine amino acids are essential for humans, but only eight of them are essential for adults (histidine is only essential for infants). Passage 2 explains that nonessential amino acids are not unnecessary, as the body can produce them from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 3 reiterates that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be produced and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "pred": "Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's production of proteins, meaning they must be obtained from the diet as the body cannot synthesize them. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body itself through processes such as converting essential amino acids or breaking down proteins. The essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, whereas nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's production of proteins, meaning they must be obtained from the diet as the body cannot synthesize them. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body itself through processes such as converting essential amino acids or breaking down proteins. The essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, whereas nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "cot": "[\"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's production of proteins, meaning they must be obtained from the diet as the body cannot synthesize them. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body itself through processes such as converting essential amino acids or breaking down proteins. The essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, whereas nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\"]", "time_sec": 14.644, "generation_time_seconds": 14.0348, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6014, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7284206188086307, "g_worst_claim": 0.6604429880777994, "g_content": 1.2895283699035645, "g_traj_mean": 0.7085293531417847}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 11, 1393, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0914446609188813, "g_worst_claim": 0.9508823818630643, "g_content": 1.6753585452125186, "g_traj_mean": 1.0758631229400635}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 525, 16587, 369, 279, 2487, 594, 5670, 315, 27796, 11, 7290, 807, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 438, 279, 2487, 4157, 6782, 26887, 1105, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 5086, 1526, 11364, 1741, 438, 33437, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 14719, 1495, 27796, 13, 576, 7565, 41400, 32869, 2924, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 11, 19853, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 1392, 82234, 11, 62481, 68, 482, 11, 34069, 19991, 11, 71057, 482, 11, 39445, 410, 482, 11, 462, 1056, 11, 1420, 482, 11, 323, 13580, 3630, 482, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids are crucial for the body's production of proteins, meaning they must be obtained from the diet as the body cannot synthesize them. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body itself through processes such as converting essential amino acids or breaking down proteins. The essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan, whereas nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.4495254237635606, "g_worst_claim": 0.02584741512934367, "g_content": 0.9977554321289063, "g_traj_mean": 0.4561748802661896}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [37438, 2283, 41400, 32869, 4157, 387, 1865, 553, 279, 2487, 11, 773, 807, 1969, 387, 12180, 504, 279, 9968, 13, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 8947, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 7565, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 13, 151645], "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9661445076388772, "g_worst_claim": 0.20888561672634548, "g_content": 1.579775129045759, "g_traj_mean": 1.0376688241958618}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.09, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "96", "question": "typical weather in indiana", "gt": "The typical weather in Indiana is characterized by long, warm, humid, and wet summers. The winters are chilly and windy with some snowfall. The temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F and is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F. The state also experiences some rainfall, with the wettest year recording 57.65 inches of rain and the driest 24.97 inches. The most snowfall recorded in a season was 74.5 inches.", "pred": "Hot, Humid Weather in Indiana", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Hot, Humid Weather in Indiana", "cot": "[\"Based on the provided passages, the typical weather in Indiana can be described as follows:\\n\\n1. **Summers**: Long, warm, humid, and wet.\\n2. **Winters**: Chilly and windy.\\n3. **Year-round**: Partly cloudy.\\n\\nSpecific details include:\\n- Temperatures typically vary from 22°F to 85°F.\\n- The temperature is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\\n- The wettest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1876 with 57.65 inches of measured rain.\\n- The driest year recorded in Indianapolis was 1934 with 24.97 inches of measured rain.\\n- The most snowfall recorded in one season was 74.5 inches in 1979 in Rockford.\\n- The maximum monthly precipitation was over 16 inches in July 2003 and 2004 at Kokomo.\\n\\nAnswer: Typical weather in Indiana includes long, warm, humid summers; chilly, windy winters; and partly cloudy conditions year-round.\", \"Typical weather in Indiana includes hot, humid summers and chilly, windy winters. The average temperature typically varies from 22°F to 85°F, and it is rarely below 3°F or above 92°F.\", \"Hot, Humid Weather in Indiana\", \"typical weather in Indiana: Hot, Humid Weather in Indiana\"]", "time_sec": 13.881, "generation_time_seconds": 13.1259, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7487, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [