, 438, 5871, 311, 7269, 31273, 323, 13403, 382, 1359, 2701, 1493, 7354, 11, 498, 646, 1855, 264, 2797, 323, 7373, 3619, 4771, 429, 13444, 91506, 279, 821, 369, 697, 3412, 13, 151645], "pred": "By following these steps, you can create a clear and effective bar graph that effectively communicates the data for your research.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.2647179631329141, "g_worst_claim": -0.05163153318258432, "g_content": 0.32084279860893306, "g_traj_mean": 0.2594998776912689}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1281, 264, 4771, 369, 697, 3412, 11, 498, 1265, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 98881, 389, 279, 943, 315, 4771, 498, 1366, 311, 990, 13, 1416, 498, 525, 26297, 2155, 11059, 11, 264, 3619, 4771, 1035, 387, 8311, 13, 1416, 498, 525, 9027, 264, 9149, 916, 882, 11, 264, 1555, 4771, 1035, 387, 2664, 624, 17, 13, 20513, 697, 821, 13, 7405, 2704, 311, 6530, 821, 429, 646, 387, 6707, 7707, 4221, 11059, 624, 18, 13, 22201, 264, 4771, 5567, 421, 498, 10702, 311, 1281, 264, 1424, 1737, 67568, 4771, 13, 1416, 498, 525, 1667, 264, 6366, 11, 5157, 279, 8311, 3162, 369, 4771, 287, 624, 19, 13, 5145, 553, 54302, 279, 4771, 448, 264, 2265, 429, 29257, 16555, 1128, 279, 4771, 374, 9027, 624, 20, 13, 29901, 279, 5452, 315, 279, 379, 35321, 13, 1096, 686, 6761, 389, 279, 2088, 315, 2750, 304, 697, 821, 624, 21, 13, 4230, 279, 15904, 476, 5128, 304, 21117, 311, 279, 821, 2750, 13, 1096, 686, 2968, 498, 264, 2797, 9124, 13042, 315, 697, 1995, 624, 22, 13, 2691, 7987, 311, 279, 15904, 476, 5128, 311, 1281, 279, 4771, 803, 42295, 32252, 323, 8661, 311, 3535, 624, 23, 13, 10294, 697, 4771, 311, 1281, 2704, 432, 29257, 10868, 697, 821, 323, 374, 4135, 311, 1349, 13, 1416, 432, 594, 537, 2797, 3322, 11, 498, 1231, 1184, 311, 7500, 279, 5452, 476, 912, 803, 7716, 13, 151645], "pred": "To make a graph for your research, you should follow these steps:\n1. Decide on the type of graph you want to use. If you are comparing different categories, a bar graph would be appropriate. If you are showing a trend over time, a line graph would be better.\n2. Collect your data. Make sure to collect data that can be easily compared among categories.\n3. Choose a graph paper if you prefer to make a hand-drawn graph. If you are using a computer, choose the appropriate software for graphing.\n4. Start by labeling the graph with a title that accurately describes what the graph is showing.\n5. Determine the scale of the y-axis. This will depend on the range of values in your data.\n6. Create the bars or lines in proportion to the data values. This will give you a clear visual representation of your information.\n7. Add colors to the bars or lines to make the graph more visually appealing and easier to understand.\n8. Review your graph to make sure it accurately represents your data and is easy to read. If it's not clear enough, you may need to adjust the scale or add more detail.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.4259997363379883, "g_worst_claim": -0.05507907697132656, "g_content": 0.5282382073803483, "g_traj_mean": 0.41019365191459656}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 17.48, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "169", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a bar graph for your research:\n\n1. Collect your data: The first step is to gather all the data you want to represent in your graph. Make sure the data is organized and easy to compare.\n2. Decide on the x-axis and y-axis: The x-axis typically represents the category or group being compared, while the y-axis represents the value or amount of each category.\n3. Determine the height of the graph: The height of the graph will depend on the highest value in your data set. Make sure to choose a height that allows for clear representation of all the data points.\n4. Create the bars: Use rectangular bars to represent each category, making sure the height of each bar is proportional to the value it represents.\n5. Add labels and titles: Label each bar with its corresponding category and add a title to the graph that describes the data being represented.\n6. Consider using a pie chart: If you have only a few data points to display and each one is substantially different in scale, a pie chart might be a better option.\n7. Format and refine: Make sure the graph is visually appealing and easy to read. Adjust the scaling of the y-axis if needed, and consider adding colors or shading to make the graph more engaging.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to create a clear and effective bar graph for your research. Good luck!", "pred": "By following these steps, you can create a bar graph that effectively represents your research data and helps in comparing different categories.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "By following these steps, you can create a bar graph that effectively represents your research data and helps in comparing different categories.", "cot": "[\"To make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Collect Your Data**: Gather all the data you need to represent. For example, if you're comparing rainfall in a city over two years, collect the rainfall data for each month.\\n\\n2. **Choose the Type of Graph**: Decide whether a bar graph or a line graph is more appropriate. Bar graphs are good for comparing groups or types, while line graphs are better for showing trends over time.\\n\\n3. **Title Your Graph**: Give your graph a clear title that describes what it represents. For instance, if you're comparing rainfall, the title could be \\\"Rainfall Comparison: February 2005 vs. February 2006\\\".\\n\\n4. **Determine the Scale**: Decide how high your graph needs to go. This will depend on the range of your data. For example, if your rainfall data ranges from 0 to 10 inches, your y-axis might go from 0 to 12 inches to allow for a clear visualization.\\n\\n5. **Create the Bars**: Draw bars for each category. The height of each bar should be proportionate to the value it represents. For example, if February 2005 had 5 inches of rainfall and February 2006 had 7 inches, the bar for February 2006 should be taller than the bar for February 2005.\\n\\n6. **Add Labels and Colors**: Label each bar with the corresponding category (e.g., \\\"February 2005\\\" and \\\"February 2006\\\"). You can also add colors to make the graph more visually appealing.\\n\\n7. **Review and Adjust**: Check your graph to ensure that the data is clearly represented and that the graph is easy to understand. Make any necessary adjustments to improve clarity.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can create a clear and effective graph to represent your research data.\", \"To make a graph for your research, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Identify the Type of Graph**: Determine the type of graph that best suits your data. Common types include bar graphs, line graphs, and pie charts.\\n\\n2. **Collect Data**: Gather the data you need to represent in the graph. Ensure the data is accurate and relevant to your research questions.\\n\\n3. **Title the Graph**: Give your graph a clear and descriptive title that explains what the graph is about.\\n\\n4. **Determine the Scale**: Decide on the scale for the y-axis, ensuring it accurately represents the range of values in your data. Adjust the scale as necessary to avoid distortion due to very disproportionate responses.\\n\\n5. **Create the Graph**: Use graph paper or a graphing tool to create the actual graph. Make sure each bar's height is proportionate to the size of the category it represents. If using a bar graph, color the bars to add visual appeal.\\n\\n6. **Review and Refine**: Check your graph for clarity and accuracy. Adjust any elements that are unclear or misleading.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can create a graph that effectively communicates your research findings.\", \"Based on the information provided:\\n\\n1. You should first collect all the data relevant to your research.\\n2. Decide on the type of graph you want to create. The passage mentions that a bar graph is useful for showing comparisons among categories, such as the amount of rainfall during different months or average salaries in different states.\\n3. If you decide to use a bar graph, title your graph and determine the height of the graph based on the data you have collected.\\n4. Make sure each bar's height represents the size of the category it corresponds to. This will provide a clear and visual representation of your data.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can create a bar graph that effectively represents your research data and helps in comparing different categories.\", \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"]", "time_sec": 31.038, "generation_time_seconds": 29.8672, "selection_time_seconds": 1.1656, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 1281, 264, 4771, 369, 697, 3412, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 47504, 4615, 2885, 9551