f the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n3. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\n4. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n5. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n6. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n7. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\n8. The other end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\n9. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n10. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\n11. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.0825635104670672, "g_worst_claim": 0.44909894466400146, "g_content": 2.679277064436573, "g_traj_mean": 1.916088581085205}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 9.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.4, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "225", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to fix a lift valve in a toilet:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet before starting the repair (Passage 1).\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms, and remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm (Passage 1).\n3. Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If necessary, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links (Passage 2).\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm (Passage 3). Secure it using pliers to ensure it is tightly fastened.\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test the toilet's flush function. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm (Passage 3).\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide instructions on how to fix a specific issue with the lift valve, such as a stuck or leaking valve.", "pred": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\nStep 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\nStep 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\nStep 3: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\nStep 5: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\nStep 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\nStep 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\nStep 3: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\nStep 5: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "cot": "[\"To fix the lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Shut off the water supply valve**: Locate the water supply valve under the sink or behind the toilet and turn it clockwise to shut off the water supply to the toilet.\\n\\n2. **Remove the old flapper valve**: Carefully remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Be sure to remove any broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n\\n3. **Prepare the new lift chain**: If necessary, adjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. This ensures the chain is the correct length for the new flapper valve.\\n\\n4. **Install the new flapper valve**: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end of the lift arm will hang freely in the air.\\n\\n5. **Connect the new lift chain**: Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n\\n6. **Turn on the water supply**: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\\n\\n7. **Check for correct operation**: Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can effectively fix the lift valve in your toilet.\", \"The passage does not provide a complete step-by-step guide on fixing a lift valve in a toilet. However, it does mention the following steps:\\n\\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\\n3. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\\n5. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\\n6. Readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\\n7. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\\n8. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\\n9. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\\n10. Use a screw (if included) and screw it in tightly.\\n11. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\\n\\nThe passage does not complete the full process of fixing the lift valve, but it provides some key steps for this process.\", \"Step 1: Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\\nStep 2: Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\nStep 4: Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\\nStep 3: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\nStep 5: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\", \"Step 1: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\\n\\nStep 2: Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n\\nStep 3: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\"]", "time_sec": 35.366, "generation_time_seconds": 34.5736, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7854, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 5046, 279, 11893, 31430, 304, 264, 26214, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 2016, 332, 1007, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 95518, 95210, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 1212, 279, 19309, 476, 4815, 279, 26214, 323, 2484, 432, 65670, 311, 9311,