{"id": "244", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "gt": "The FEHA (Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) have several differences. The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another important difference is the requirement of disability. The ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity, while the FEHA only requires that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, without the need for it to be a substantial limit.", "pred": "There are several differences between the FEHA (the Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act). One difference is that the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. Another difference is that with FEHA, a disability is required, but under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "There are several differences between the FEHA (the Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act). One difference is that the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. Another difference is that with FEHA, a disability is required, but under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.", "cot": "[\"The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn’t distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\", \"One difference between the FEHA and the ADA is that the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees and doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. Another difference is that with FEHA, a disability is required, meaning a mental and physical disability must limit a major life activity. Under the ADA, to qualify for disability, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity.\", \"There are several differences between the FEHA (the Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act). One difference is that the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. Another difference is that with FEHA, a disability is required, but under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\", \"One key difference between the FEHA and ADA is the minimum number of employees covered. The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees and doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. Also, FEHA requires only that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.\"]", "time_sec": 15.403, "generation_time_seconds": 14.5723, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8252, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 78325, 16790, 311, 678, 22426, 304, 279, 869, 10486, 429, 614, 3040, 311, 36655, 8256, 11, 19853, 279, 27931, 17020, 21501, 803, 8256, 11, 323, 