{"id": "246", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) are:\n\n1. Scope of application: The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\n2. Definition of disability: The FEHA requires that a disability must limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires that a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\n3. Number of employees: The ADA applies to employers with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA has no such requirement.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between FEHA and ADA is the scope of application and the definition of disability.", "pred": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn’t distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn’t distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.", "cot": "[\"The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn’t distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\", \"The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects a larger number of employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas FEHA requires only that a disability limits a major life activity.\", \"FEHA and ADA have significant differences. FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. FEHA requires a disability, while the ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.\", \"FEHA applies to more employees than ADA does. FEHA does not distinguish between private and public employers, while ADA does. FEHA requires a disability, whereas ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.\"]", "time_sec": 12.04, "generation_time_seconds": 11.4914, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5397, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 78325, 16790, 311, 678, 22426, 304, 279, 869, 10486, 429, 614, 3040, 311, 36655, 8256, 11, 19853, 279, 27931, 17020, 21501, 803, 8256, 11, 323, 432, 3171, 1405, 32037, 1948, 869, 323, 584, 22426, 1075, 279, 78325, 1558, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn’t distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.574187640582814, "g_worst_claim": 1.628350067138672, "g_content": 3.4195632934570312, "g_traj_mean": 2.540234327316284}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 78325, 16790, 311, 678, 22426, 304, 279, 869, 10486, 448, 3040, 311, 36655, 8256, 11, 19853, 279, 27931, 17020, 21501, 264, 8131, 1372, 315, 8256, 323, 1558, 537, 32037, 1948, 869, 323, 584, 22426, 13, 22406, 11, 1212, 279, 78325, 11, 264, 6961, 476, 10502, 52217, 1969, 31202, 3930, 264, 3598, 2272, 5702, 311, 25384, 438, 264, 27254, 11, 19853, 27931, 17020, 7460, 1172, 429, 264, 27254, 13388, 264, 3598, 2272, 5702, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects a larger number of employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas FEHA requires only that a disability limits a major life activity.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.722860186062162, "g_worst_claim": 0.9185462527804904, "g_content": 1.9948546950881545, "g_traj_mean": 1.6134859323501587}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [11419, 17020, 323, 78325, 614, 5089, 11799, 13, 27931, 17020, 16790, 311, 803, 8256, 323, 1558, 537, 32037, 1948, 869, 323, 584, 22426, 1075, 279, 78325, 1558, 13, 27931, 17020, 7460, 264, 27254, 11, 1393, 279, 78325, 7460, 264, 6961, 476, 10502, 52217, 429, 31202, 13388, 264, 3598, 2272, 5702, 13, 151645], "pred": "FEHA and ADA have significant differences. FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does. FEHA requires a disability, while the ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3895947907485213, "g_worst_claim": 0.34749146068797393, "g_content": 1.4280643463134766, "g_traj_mean": 1.1728448867797852}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [11419, 17020, 16790, 311, 803, 8256, 1091, 78325, 1558, 13, 27931, 17020, 1558, 537, 32037, 1948, 869, 323, 584, 22426, 11, 1393, 78325, 1558, 13, 27931, 17020, 7460, 264, 27254, 11, 19853, 78325, 7460, 264, 6961, 476, 10502, 52217, 429, 31202, 13388, 264, 3598, 2272, 5702, 13, 151645], "pred": "FEHA applies to more employees than ADA does. FEHA does not distinguish between private and public employers, while ADA does. FEHA requires a disability, whereas ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.", "grounding_scores": 