{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun, starting with a relative pronoun like who, whose, whom, which, or that, or a subordinate conjunction like when and where. The main difference lies in what they modify and how they are introduced.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun, starting with a relative pronoun like who, whose, whom, which, or that, or a subordinate conjunction like when and where. The main difference lies in what they modify and how they are introduced.", "cot": "[\"An adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by a subordinate conjunction such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).\", \"An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. It is introduced by a subordinate conjunction such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, or while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or a pronoun. It begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).\", \"An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun, starting with a relative pronoun like who, whose, whom, which, or that, or a subordinate conjunction like when and where. The main difference lies in what they modify and how they are introduced.\", \"An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while.\\n\\nAn adjective clause, on the other hand, is a dependent clause that modifies a noun or a pronoun. It typically begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).\"]", "time_sec": 20.434, "generation_time_seconds": 19.5844, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8403, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [2082, 993, 22328, 21647, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 279, 18607, 11, 323, 374, 11523, 553, 264, 78163, 31446, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 