ronoun. It begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1381974760230844, "g_worst_claim": 0.1649491970355694, "g_content": 1.3099586963653564, "g_traj_mean": 0.9061494469642639}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2082, 993, 22328, 21647, 374, 264, 17749, 21647, 429, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 46041, 279, 18607, 13, 2410, 22328, 49098, 525, 11523, 553, 78163, 31446, 82, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 323, 1393, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 374, 1483, 311, 5602, 264, 36921, 476, 264, 18613, 1624, 11, 5916, 448, 264, 8674, 18613, 1624, 1075, 879, 11, 6693, 11, 8711, 11, 892, 11, 476, 429, 11, 476, 264, 78163, 31446, 1075, 979, 323, 1380, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 15448, 304, 1128, 807, 5602, 323, 1246, 807, 525, 11523, 13, 151645], "pred": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. Adverb clauses are introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, is used to modify a noun or a pronoun, starting with a relative pronoun like who, whose, whom, which, or that, or a subordinate conjunction like when and where. The main difference lies in what they modify and how they are introduced.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0262910879072464, "g_worst_claim": 0.38471783910478863, "g_content": 1.3658236503601073, "g_traj_mean": 0.8301336765289307}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2082, 993, 22328, 21647, 374, 264, 17749, 21647, 429, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 46041, 279, 18607, 13