{"id": "359", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument made by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered and redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties. In summary, both types of attacks involve attacking the person making the argument, but poisoning the well fallacy specifically takes place before the merits of a case are considered.", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "cot": "[\"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy attacks an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, the poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that targets a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. An example of this is when someone dismisses another person's argument solely because of their background or beliefs.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, involves attacking the integrity or intelligence of one of the parties before the merits of the case can be considered. This fallacy redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\\n\\nThe key difference between the two is that the ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy attacks the person's integrity or intelligence before the argument can be evaluated.\", \"Both are ad hominem fallacies, which are attacks on a person rather than on their argument. However, ad hominem is an attack on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is an attack on the person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered.\"]", "time_sec": 17.929, "generation_time_seconds": 17.1095, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8126, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279,