ies.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4427725077548743, "g_worst_claim": 0.473466157913208, "g_content": 2.3153082529703775, "g_traj_mean": 1.4114106893539429}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 979, 4325, 13432, 288, 2441, 1697, 594, 5693, 21063, 1576, 315, 862, 4004, 476, 20799, 382, 785, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 17601, 22818, 279, 16614, 476, 11229, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 279, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1096, 4399, 2757, 71171, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 382, 785, 1376, 6672, 1948, 279, 1378, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 8776, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 8776, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 5693, 646, 387, 25070, 13, 151645], "pred": "The key difference between the two is that the ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy attacks the person's integrity or intelligence before the argument can be evaluated.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7756978933526066, "g_worst_claim": 0.08180384039878845, "g_content": 1.2118991728751891, "g_traj_mean": 0.7321112751960754}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [20629, 525, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 26030, 11, 892, 525, 8776, 389, 264, 1697, 4751, 1091, 389, 862, 5693, 13, 4354, 11, 993, 4998, 68413, 374, 458, 3359, 389, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 1393, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 3359, 389, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 151645], "pred": "Both are ad hominem fallacies, which are attacks on a person rather than on their argument. However, ad hominem is an attack on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is an attack on the person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8178592467273789, "g_worst_claim": 0.12869090835253397, "g_content": 1.696294931265024, "g_traj_mean": 0.9816195964813232}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.54, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "360", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy:\n\nAd hominem fallacy refers to attacking an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, and it redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\n\nIn other words, ad hominem fallacy is a broader term that encompasses various types of attacks on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that focuses on the person's integrity or intelligence.", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument instead of addressing the argument itself. It includes attacks on the person's traits as a way to invalidate their arguments. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar in that it's an ad hominem attack, but specifically targets the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than focusing on the argument at hand.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument instead of addressing the argument itself. It includes attacks on the person's traits as a way to invalidate their arguments. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar in that it's an ad hominem attack, but specifically targets the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than focusing on the argument at hand.", "cot": "[\"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the character of another person instead of addressing the actual argument. In contrast, the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before they can present their case, thereby invalidating their argument before it can be considered. An example of this is when someone attacks another person's credibility or intelligence before they have the chance to present their argument.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument instead of addressing the argument itself. It includes attacks on the person's traits as a way to invalidate their arguments. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar in that it's an ad hominem attack, but specifically targets the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than focusing on the argument at hand.\", \"Ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. Poisoning the well fallacy occurs when an ad hominem attack is used that redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties before the merits of a case can be considered.\"]", "time_sec": 16.413, "generation_time_seconds": 15.8499, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5563, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 979, 4325, 8776, 279, 1697, 594, 11229, 476, 16614, 1573, 24392, 279, 49832, 315, 862, 5693, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 4428, 11, 714, 432, 13666, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4104752827750764, "g_worst_claim": 0.31985945171780056, "g_content": 2.179599962736431, "g_traj_mean": 1.3871031999588013}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 3359, 389, 458, 5693, 553, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 7035, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 979, 4325, 8776, 279, 3668, 315, 2441, 1697, 4518, 315, 27020, 279, 5042, 5693, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 807, 646, 3042, 862, 1142, 11, 27492, 8318, 1095, 862, 5693, 1573, 432, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 979, 4325, 8776, 2441, 1697, 594, 37669, 476, 11229, 1573, 807, 614, 279, 6012, 311, 3042, 862, 5693, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the character of another person instead of addressing the actual argument. In contrast, the poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before they can present their case, thereby invalidating their argument before it can be considered. An example of this is when someone attacks another person's credibility or intelligence before they have the chance to present their argument.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.5858539880187207, "g_worst_claim": 0.022624234358469646, "g_content": 1.0544195556640625, "g_traj_mean": 0.5732932686805725}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4518, 315, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 1084, 5646, 8776, 389, 279, 1697, 594, 24055, 438, 264, 1616, 311, 39943, 862, 5977, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 4428, 304, 429, 432, 594, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 11, 714, 11689, 11550, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 11, 4751, 1091, 21080, 389, 279, 5693, 518, 1424, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument instead of addressing the argument itself. It includes attacks on the person's traits as a way to invalidate their arguments. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar in that it's an ad hominem attack, but specifically targets the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than focusing on the argument at hand.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1689592459300553, "g_worst_claim": 0.10236881176630656, "g_content": 1.977629288383152, "g_traj_mean": 1.1905823945999146}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2589, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 51112, 287, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 13666, 979, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 374, 1483, 429, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 151645], "pred": "Ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. Poisoning the well fallacy occurs when an ad hominem attack is used that redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties before the merits of a case can be considered.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8327073988767204, "g_worst_claim": -0.2915388175419399, "g_content": 1.7576932599467616, "g_traj_mean": 1.1643545627593994}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 53.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "361", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "The main difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is the timing of the attack. Ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument instead of attacking the argument directly, but poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive attack on a person's character or integrity before they have even made their argument. In other words, ad hominem fallacy is a reaction to an argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive strike to discredit an opponent before they have a chance to present their argument.", "pred": "Both the ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy are aimed at discrediting an argument indirectly by attacking the person rather than addressing the argument itself. The main difference lies in the timing of the attack: the ad hominem fallacy can occur at any point during a discussion, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens specifically at the beginning of a discussion.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Both the ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy are aimed at discrediting an argument indirectly by attacking the person rather than addressing the argument itself. The main difference lies in the timing of the attack: the ad hominem fallacy can occur at any point during a discussion, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens specifically at the beginning of a discussion.", "cot": "[\"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the person making the argument rather than the argument itself, while the poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. It is a means to invalidate an argument by attacking the person, rather than the argument itself.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy, also an ad hominem attack, involves redirecting a discussion to the faults of one of the parties before the merits of a case can be considered. It's a strategy to undermine an argument by attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before their case can be evaluated.\\n\\nBoth the ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy are aimed at discrediting an argument indirectly by attacking the person rather than addressing the argument itself. The main difference lies in the timing of the attack: the ad hominem fallacy can occur at any point during a discussion, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens specifically at the beginning of a discussion.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. It is often used as a way to invalidate the argument by attacking the person making it. For example, if someone is arguing about the effectiveness of a new drug, a person using the ad hominem fallacy might attack the pharmaceutical company that produced the drug rather than discussing the effectiveness of the drug itself.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is an ad hominem attack on a person’s integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties. For example, if someone is trying to prove that a politician is honest, another person might attack the politician's character before the politician has a chance to present their case. This can make the audience more likely to accept the attack on the politician's character rather than consider the politician's case on its own merits.\"]", "time_sec": 21.466, "generation_time_seconds": 20.6674, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7947, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 979, 4325, 8776, 279, 1697, 594, 11229, 476, 16614, 1573, 24392, 279, 49832, 315, 862, 5693, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 4428, 11, 714, 432, 13666, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is when someone attacks the person's intelligence or integrity before discussing the merits of their argument. The poisoning the well fallacy is similar, but it occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3964196999330905, "g_worst_claim": 0.28387461768256295, "g_content": 2.1287066811009456, "g_traj_mean": 1.3748102188110352}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 279, 5693, 5086, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 151645], "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the person making the argument rather than the argument itself, while the poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0467197150514838, "g_worst_claim": 0.5434724908126028, "g_content": 1.5738371144170347, "g_traj_mean": 1.1278432607650757}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 1084, 374, 264, 3363, 311, 39943, 458, 5693, 553, 22818, 279, 1697, 11, 4751, 1091, 279, 5693, 5086, 382, 785, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 11, 1083, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 11, 17601, 6423, 287, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 594, 264, 8282, 311, 41947, 458, 5693, 553, 22818, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 1573, 862, 1142, 646, 387, 25070, 382, 20629, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 323, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 525, 19469, 518, 827, 10844, 5853, 458, 5693, 45245, 553, 22818, 279, 1697, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 1887, 6672, 15448, 304, 279, 18405, 315, 279, 3359, 25, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 646, 12170, 518, 894, 1459, 2337, 264, 10219, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 8573, 11689, 518, 279, 7167, 315, 264, 10219, 13, 151645], "pred": "Both the ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy are aimed at discrediting an argument indirectly by attacking the person rather than addressing the argument itself. The main difference lies in the timing of the attack: the ad hominem fallacy can occur at any point during a discussion, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens specifically at the beginning of a discussion.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.80