7761, 320, 67, 449, 48765, 14212, 43636, 568, 151645], "pred": "In summary, twins can happen either when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos (monozygotic twins) or when two different eggs are fertilized by different sperm cells (dizygotic twins).", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.8155753932463211, "g_worst_claim": -0.022877030074596405, "g_content": 0.958848135811942, "g_traj_mean": 0.6559784412384033}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.03, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "429", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Twins can happen in two ways: monozygotic (identical) twins occur when one zygote splits into two embryos, while dizygotic (fraternal) twins happen when two different eggs are fertilized by two separate sperm cells. The likelihood of dizygotic twins is influenced by factors such as West African descent, being between 30 and 40 years old, being taller and heavier than average, and having had several previous pregnancies. Conjoined twins occur when the division of the zygote happens within 8 days of fertilization and can result in complications due to shared organs.", "pred": "[Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.]", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "[Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.]", "cot": "[\"Twins can happen in two main ways: monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.\", \"Twins can happen in two ways: monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal).\\n\\nIdentical twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. This splitting occurs after the ninth day after fertilization. An example of identical twins is conjoined twins, where the division of the developing zygote into two embryos occurs within 8 days of fertilization. Conjoined twins have a high mortality rate due to complications from shared organs.\\n\\nFraternal twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Dizygotic twins are slightly more likely to occur when the woman is of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the age of 30 and 40 years, greater than average height and weight, or has had several previous pregnancies. An example of fraternal twins is a pair of twins where each twin is fertilized by a different sperm cell.\\n\\nIn summary, twins happen when two offspring are produced from the same pregnancy. Twins can be identical or fraternal, with identical twins being a result of one zygote splitting after fertilization, and fraternal twins being produced from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.\", \"Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.\\n\\nAnswer:[Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.]\", \"Twins can happen in two ways, depending on the development of the zygote:\\n\\n1. Monozygotic (identical) twins: This occurs when a single zygote splits into two embryos after fertilization. This happens after the ninth day of fertilization, according to the first passage.\\n\\n2. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins: This type of twinning happens when two separate eggs are fertilized by different sperm cells, resulting in two embryos. This is more common and is influenced by certain factors in the woman, as described in the second passage.\\n\\nBoth types of twins can result in twins, but monozygotic twins are less common, occurring about 1 in 250 pregnancies, while dizygotic twins are more common, occurring about 1 in 80 pregnancies.\"]", "time_sec": 24.269, "generation_time_seconds": 23.321, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9389, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 1887, 5510, 25, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 323, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 568, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 11, 1393, 42286, 48765, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 11, 1817, 35114, 1506, 553, 1181, 1828, 39599, 2779, 13, 151645], "pred": "Twins can happen in two main ways: monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7423061962357398, "g_worst_claim": 0.6322343999689276, "g_content": 1.1369652218288846, "g_traj_mean": 0.7181006669998169}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 5510, 25, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 3593, 28301, 938, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 13, 1096, 44373, 13666, 1283, 279, 42541, 1899, 1283, 35114, 2022, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 19516, 43636, 374, 390, 71154, 43636, 11, 1380, 279, 12804, 315, 279, 11220, 1147, 48765, 1272, 1119, 1378, 88773, 13666, 2878, 220, 23, 2849, 315, 35114, 2022, 13, 1200, 71154, 43636, 614, 264, 1550, 28428, 4379, 4152, 311, 35405, 504, 6094, 35753, 382, 37, 17606, 2978, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 11, 1817, 35114, 1506, 553, 1181, 1828, 39599, 2779, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 43636, 525, 10078, 803, 4363, 311, 12170, 979, 279, 5220, 374, 315, 4312, 11643, 36952, 320, 34946, 809, 269, 30429, 701, 1948, 279, 4231, 315, 220, 18, 15, 323, 220, 19, 15, 1635, 11, 7046, 1091, 5461, 2608, 323, 4680, 11, 476, 702, 1030, 3807, 3681, 81208, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 80972, 2978, 43636, 374, 264, 6716, 315, 43636, 1380, 1817, 27397, 374, 35114, 1506, 553, 264, 2155, 39599, 2779, 382, 641, 12126, 11, 43636, 3537, 979, 1378, 45371, 525, 8947, 504, 279, 1852, 19636, 13, 65268, 646, 387, 19516, 476, 80972, 2978, 11, 448, 19516, 43636, 1660, 264, 1102, 315, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 44373, 1283, 35114, 2022, 11, 323, 80972, 2978, 43636, 1660, 8947, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 35114, 1506, 553, 8651, 39599, 7761, 13, 151645], "pred": "In summary, twins happen when two offspring are produced from the same pregnancy. Twins can be identical or fraternal, with identical twins being a result of one zygote splitting after fertilization, and fraternal twins being produced from two different eggs fertilized by separate sperm cells.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0881670663145766, "g_worst_claim": -0.19451867043972015, "g_content": 1.5113222915514382, "g_traj_mean": 1.077375888824463}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 387, 2987, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 568, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 11, 1817, 35114, 1506, 553, 1181, 1828, 39599, 2779, 382, 16141, 7259, 22816, 1330, 646, 387, 2987, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 568, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 11, 1817, 35114, 1506, 553, 1181, 1828, 39599, 2779, 24719, 151645], "pred": "[Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos. Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.]", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.38939336915203543, "g_worst_claim": -0.0002987717485262288, "g_content": 0.7083161801707988, "g_traj_mean": 0.3711659014225006}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 5510, 11, 11649, 389, 279, 4401, 315, 279, 1147, 48765, 1272, 1447, 16, 13, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 43636, 25, 1096, 13666, 979, 264, 3175, 1147, 48765, 1272, 40467, 1119, 1378, 88773, 1283, 35114, 2022, 13, 1096, 8573, 1283, 279, 42541, 1899, 315, 35114, 2022, 11, 4092, 311, 279, 1156, 21085, 382, 17, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 8, 43636, 25, 1096, 943, 315, 4384, 19770, 8573, 979, 1378, 8651, 18805, 525, 35114, 1506, 553, 2155, 39599, 7761, 11, 12942, 304, 1378, 88773, 13, 1096, 374, 803, 4185, 323, 374, 27061, 553, 3654, 9363, 304, 279, 5220, 11, 438, 7481, 304, 279, 2086, 21085, 382, 20629, 4494, 315, 43636, 646, 1102, 304, 43636, 11, 714, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 525, 2686, 4185, 11, 30865, 911, 220, 16, 304, 220, 17, 20, 15, 81208, 11, 1393, 42286, 48765, 14212, 43636, 525, 803, 4185, 11, 30865, 911, 220, 16, 304, 220, 23, 15, 81208, 13, 151645], "pred": "Both types of twins can result in twins, but monozygotic twins are less common, occurring about 1 in 250 pregnancies, while dizygotic twins are more common, occurring about 1 in 80 pregnancies.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.6401534236592826, "g_worst_claim": -0.055716675519943235, "g_content": 0.87211872549618, "g_traj_mean": 0.5070735812187195}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 38.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "430", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how twins happen:\n\n* Monoamniotic twins: These twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\n* Conjoined twins: When the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs, it happens within 8 days of fertilization, and mortality is highest for conjoined twins due to the many complications resulting from shared organs.\n* Dizygotic twin pregnancies: These twins are slightly more likely to occur in women who are of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, are taller and heavier than average, and have had several previous pregnancies.\n* Identical twins (monozygotic): These twins develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos.\n* Fraternal twins (dizygotic): These twins develop from two different eggs, and each twin is fertilized by its own sperm cell.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages: The passage does not provide information on the exact timing or mechanism of twinning beyond what is mentioned above.", "pred": "Twins can happen in two ways, depending on genetic and environmental factors. Monozygotic (identical) twins occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos, resulting in two babies that are genetically identical. This typically happens within the first 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins occur when two separate eggs are fertilized by different sperm, resulting in two babies who are genetically unrelated but may share some physical or genetic similarities. Factors such as the woman's age, ethnicity, and weight can affect the likelihood of dizygotic twins.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Twins can happen in two ways, depending on genetic and environmental factors. Monozygotic (identical) twins occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos, resulting in two babies that are genetically identical. This typically happens within the first 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins occur when two separate eggs are fertilized by different sperm, resulting in two babies who are genetically unrelated but may share some physical or genetic similarities. Factors such as the woman's age, ethnicity, and weight can affect the likelihood of dizygotic twins.", "cot": "[\"Twins can happen in two main ways: monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.\", \"Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs.\", \"Twins can happen in two primary ways:\\n1. Monozygotic Twins (Identical Twins): When the fertilized egg splits after the ninth day of development, resulting in two embryos sharing the same amniotic sac. This type of twin formation is rare, occurring about 1% of the time. An example of monozygotic twins is conjoined twins, who are formed when the division of the early embryo occurs within 8 days of fertilization. These twins often share many organs and have a higher mortality rate due to the complications arising from their shared anatomy.\\n2. Dizygotic Twins (Fraternal Twins): When two separate eggs are fertilized by two different sperm cells. This is the most common type of twin formation, occurring about 99% of the time. Fraternal twins are genetically similar but are not identical. They can be of different sexes or the same sex. Fraternal twins are more likely to occur in women who meet certain criteria: being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.\", \"Twins can happen in two ways, depending on genetic and environmental factors. Monozygotic (identical) twins occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos, resulting in two babies that are genetically identical. This typically happens within the first 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins occur when two separate eggs are fertilized by different sperm, resulting in two babies who are genetically unrelated but may share some physical or genetic similarities. Factors such as the woman's age, ethnicity, and weight can affect the likelihood of dizygotic twins.\"]", "time_sec": 20.275, "generation_time_seconds": 19.3992, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8689, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 1887, 5510, 25, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 323, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 568, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 11, 1393, 42286, 48765, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 11, 1817, 35114, 1506, 553, 1181, 1828, 39599, 2779, 13, 151645], "pred": "Twins can happen in two main ways: monozygotic (identical) and dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7227973619355836, "g_worst_claim": 0.6116713010347806, "g_content": 0.9993167453342013, "g_traj_mean": 0.6760315299034119}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 387, 2987, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 476, 42286, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 568, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 825, 1147, 48765, 1272, 429, 40467, 323, 7586, 1378, 88773, 11, 1393, 42286, 48765, 14212, 43636, 2225, 504, 1378, 2155, 18805, 13, 151645], "pred": "Twins can be either monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7398855838711415, "g_worst_claim": 0.658508194817437, "g_content": 1.2046005942604758, "g_traj_mean": 0.7145664691925049}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 6028, 5510, 510, 16, 13, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 65268, 320, 28301, 938, 65268, 1648, 3197, 279, 35114, 1506, 18636, 40467, 1283, 279, 42541, 1899, 315, 4401, 11, 12942, 304, 1378, 88773, 11560, 279, 1852, 1079, 7751, 14212, 11100, 13, 1096, 943, 315, 27397, 18003, 374, 8848, 11, 30865, 911, 220, 16, 4, 315, 279, 882, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 39674, 4246, 70, 14212, 43636, 374, 390, 71154, 43636, 11, 879, 525, 14122, 979, 279, 12804, 315, 279, 4124, 86601, 13666, 2878, 220, 23, 2849, 315, 35114, 2022, 13, 4220, 43636, 3545, 4332, 1657, 35753, 323, 614, 264, 5080, 28428, 4379, 4152, 311, 279, 35405, 39886, 504, 862, 6094, 61590, 624, 17, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 65268, 320, 37, 17606, 2978, 65268, 1648, 3197, 1378, 8651, 18805, 525, 35114, 1506, 553, 1378, 2155, 39599, 7761, 13, 1096, 374, 279, 1429, 4185, 943, 315, 27397, 18003, 11, 30865, 911, 220, 24, 24, 4, 315, 279, 882, 13, 2869, 266, 2978, 43636, 525, 50933, 4428, 714, 525, 537, 19516, 13, 2379, 646, 387, 315, 2155, 74932, 476, 279, 1852, 1839, 13, 2869, 266, 2978, 43636, 525, 803, 4363, 311, 12170, 304, 3198, 879, 3367, 3654, 12890, 25, 1660, 315, 4312, 11643, 36952, 320, 34946, 809, 269, 30429, 701, 1948, 279, 16639, 315, 220, 18, 15, 323, 220, 19, 15, 11, 7046, 1091, 5461, 2608, 323, 4680, 11, 323, 3432, 1030, 3807, 3681, 81208, 13, 151645], "pred": "Twins can happen in two primary ways:\n1. Monozygotic Twins (Identical Twins): When the fertilized egg splits after the ninth day of development, resulting in two embryos sharing the same amniotic sac. This type of twin formation is rare, occurring about 1% of the time. An example of monozygotic twins is conjoined twins, who are formed when the division of the early embryo occurs within 8 days of fertilization. These twins often share many organs and have a higher mortality rate due to the complications arising from their shared anatomy.\n2. Dizygotic Twins (Fraternal Twins): When two separate eggs are fertilized by two different sperm cells. This is the most common type of twin formation, occurring about 99% of the time. Fraternal twins are genetically similar but are not identical. They can be of different sexes or the same sex. Fraternal twins are more likely to occur in women who meet certain criteria: being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7368885366721986, "g_worst_claim": -0.09115763902664184, "g_content": 1.1987290881928943, "g_traj_mean": 0.7496437430381775}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [22816, 1330, 646, 3537, 304, 1378, 5510, 11, 11649, 389, 18929, 323, 12158, 9363, 13, 12691, 4246, 70, 14212, 320, 1713, 938, 8, 43636, 12170, 979, 264, 3175, 35114, 1506, 18636, 40467, 1119, 1378, 88773, 11, 12942, 304, 1378, 23920, 429, 525, 50933, 19516, 13, 1096, 11136, 8573, 2878, 279, 1156, 220, 23, 2849, 315, 35114, 2022, 13, 422, 449, 48765, 14212, 320, 1626, 266, 2978, 8, 43636, 12170, 979, 1378, 8651, 18805, 525, 35114, 1506, 553, 2155, 39599, 11, 12942, 304, 1378, 23920, 879, 525, 50933, 45205, 714, 1231, 4332, 1045, 6961, 476, 18929, 42774, 13, 67218, 1741, 438, 279, 5220, 594, 4231, 11, 56878, 11, 323, 4680, 646, 7802, 279, 28636, 315, 42286, 48765, 14212, 43636, 13, 151645], "pred": "Twins can happen in two ways, depending on genetic and environmental factors. Monozygotic (identical) twins occur when a single fertilized egg splits into two embryos, resulting in two babies that are genetically identical. This typically happens within the first 8 days of fertilization. Dizygotic (fraternal) twins occur when two separate eggs are fertilized by different sperm, resulting in two babies who are genetically unrelated but may share some physical or genetic similarities. Factors such as the woman's age, ethnicity, and weight can affect the likelihood of dizygotic twins.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.41517152512793687, "g_worst_claim": -0.16451120376586914, "g_content": 0.6469110187731291, "g_traj_mean": 0.34291407465934753}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "431", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first determine the extent of the tarnish. If the jewelry is slightly dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth into the solution. Rub the jewelry with the cloth, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the jewelry and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. If your silver jewelry is lightly tarnished, you can also try submerging it in a hot saltwater bath. Ensure that if your jewelry has gemstones, they won't be affected by the saltwater. For diamonds, clean with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, and pat dry with a soft cloth.", "pred": "3. If your jewelry has gemstones, use a gentle solution like saltwater to avoid affecting the gemstones. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method removes tarnish without abrading the silver.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. If your jewelry has gemstones, use a gentle solution like saltwater to avoid affecting the gemstones. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method removes tarnish without abrading the silver.", "cot": "[\"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. For items merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver.\\n\\n3. If your jewelry has gemstones, use a gentle solution like saltwater to avoid affecting the gemstones. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method removes tarnish without abrading the silver.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use various methods provided in the passages:\\n\\n1. **For Dull, Filmy, or Starting to Discolor Jewelry**: Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. **For Heavier Tarnish**: Mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and wet the silver. Apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\n3. **For Diamonds**: Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n4. **For Silver Jewelry with Gemstones**: Use a saltwater solution. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver.\\n\\nEach method is described in detail in the passages provided, ensuring that you can choose the appropriate approach based on the type of jewelry you are cleaning.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few drops of mild dish soap mixed with warm water. Dip the jewelry in the solution and rub it with a soft cloth. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth.\"]", "time_sec": 25.007, "generation_time_seconds": 24.1152, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8847, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 11, 14401, 279, 14961, 11, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 6291, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.879587734029942, "g_worst_claim": 0.4765878915786743, "g_content": 2.6216197869716544, "g_traj_mean": 1.851007342338562}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 432, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 382, 18, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 990, 264, 21700, 6291, 1075, 12021, 12987, 311, 5648, 27887, 279, 18747, 32510, 13, 25968, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 13, 1446, 1101, 1184, 3322, 311, 3421, 279, 30717, 498, 2299, 15826, 13, 1096, 1714, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 151645], "pred": "3. If your jewelry has gemstones, use a gentle solution like saltwater to avoid affecting the gemstones. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method removes tarnish without abrading the silver.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8518183453023431, "g_worst_claim": 0.22477316856384277, "g_content": 2.413846102627841, "g_traj_mean": 1.7663238048553467}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86