ed": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few drops of mild dish soap mixed with warm water. Dip the jewelry in the solution and rub it with a soft cloth. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.195086932082722, "g_worst_claim": 0.6609189169747489, "g_content": 1.4412950457948628, "g_traj_mean": 1.2034722566604614}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.97, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "432", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods based on the passages provided. For silver jewelry, you can mix mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth into the mixture, and rub the jewelry. Then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, apply it to the silver with a soft cloth, and rinse it off. For diamonds, you can clean them with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat them dry with a soft cloth. If you have silver jewelry with gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "cot": "[\"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use different methods depending on the level of tarnish. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip them in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and soak the jewelry. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mild dish soap and warm water mixture for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor. For heavier tarnish, you can create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to soak the jewelry in a bowl filled with hot water and enough to cover the jewelry being cleaned. This method should remove tarnish without damaging the silver. For heavily tarnished jewelry or when cleaning multiple pieces at once, using more water is recommended. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the cleaning process.\"]", "time_sec": 28.837, "generation_time_seconds": 27.9463, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8858, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 11, 14401, 279, 14961, 11, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 6291, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8740599810981924, "g_worst_claim": 0.45055705308914185, "g_content": 2.599254901592548, "g_traj_mean": 1.8480095863342285}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 20980, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 323, 8205, 3015, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568, 38478, 11, 498, 646, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 11, 3322, 311, 3421, 279, 30717, 498, 2299, 15826, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Alternatively, you can pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.877468143942835, "g_worst_claim": 0.22910470228928786, "g_content": 2.2925183935072817, "g_traj_mean": 1.8727037906646729}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 2155, 5413, 11649, 389, 279, 2188, 315, 86480, 812, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 1105, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 11, 14401, 279, 14961, 11, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 568, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 60692, 279, 30717, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 498, 646, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use different methods depending on the level of tarnish. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip them in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch). Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and soak the jewelry. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.922879484673057, "g_worst_claim": 0.7817416191101074, "g_content": 2.4725054750348083, "g_traj_mean": 1.8824872970581055}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 23034, 12000, 26785, 323, 8205, 3015, 20980, 369, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 1855, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 323, 3796, 432, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 60692, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 19212, 10199, 448, 4017, 3015, 323, 3322, 311, 3421, 279, 30717, 1660, 27722, 13, 1096, 1714, 1265, 4057, 86480, 812, 2041, 33346, 279, 14961, 13, 1752, 16916, 86480, 3304, 30717, 476, 979, 15826, 5248, 9666, 518, 3055, 11, 1667, 803, 3015, 374, 11102, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 5978, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 279, 15826, 1882, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mild dish soap and warm water mixture for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor. For heavier tarnish, you can create a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to soak the jewelry in a bowl filled with hot water and enough to cover the jewelry being cleaned. This method should remove tarnish without damaging the silver. For heavily tarnished jewelry or when cleaning multiple pieces at once, using more water is recommended. If your jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by the cleaning process.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5119717111199507, "g_worst_claim": 0.8427422523498536, "g_content": 2.144183114858774, "g_traj_mean": 1.6009007692337036}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 59.43, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "433", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home:\n\n1. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth to shine silver jewelry that is merely dull or starting to discolor. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n2. Use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth.\n3. Soak tarnished silver jewelry in a saltwater solution of two cups of hot water. This method is gentle and can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Make sure the gemstones will not be affected by the solution before submerging them.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to clean other types of jewelry, such as gold or platinum, or what to do if the tarnish is heavy or if the jewelry has intricate designs that may be damaged during cleaning.", "pred": "3. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "3. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "cot": "[\"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use several methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\n2. For diamonds, use warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\n3. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n3. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **For Items with Light Tarnish:**\\n   - Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water.\\n   - Dip a soft cloth in the solution and rub the jewelry.\\n   - Rinse the jewelry in cool water.\\n   - Buff the jewelry dry with a cloth.\\n\\n2. **For Heavier Tarnish:**\\n   - Mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water.\\n   - Apply the paste to the silver using a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n   - Allow the paste to sit for a while.\\n   - Rinse the jewelry in cool water.\\n   - Buff the jewelry dry with a cloth.\\n\\n3. **Using Salt:**\\n   - Add two cups of hot water to a bowl.\\n   - Submerge the jewelry in the saltwater solution.\\n   - Let the jewelry sit in the solution to remove tarnish.\\n   - If using a lot of jewelry, adjust the amount of water accordingly.\\n   - For a single piece of jewelry, use less water.\\n   - Ensure that any gemstones are not affected by the saltwater.\\n\\nThese methods provide gentle yet effective ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home.\"]", "time_sec": 33.071, "generation_time_seconds": 32.0265, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0354, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 11, 14401, 279, 14961, 11, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 6291, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8877416009001091, "g_worst_claim": 0.5646930932998657, "g_content": 2.59358156644381, "g_traj_mean": 1.8473701477050781}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 3807, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 48051, 11, 990, 8205, 773, 9667, 3015, 323, 264, 8413, 30117, 28300, 15625, 476, 2310, 25507, 36061, 13, 7135, 432, 9058, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 382, 18, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 13, 1446, 1101, 1184, 3322, 311, 3421, 279, 30717, 498, 2299, 15826, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 498, 646, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "3. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.9673330579897328, "g_worst_claim": 0.588783597946167, "g_content": 2.3262238084224234, "g_traj_mean": 1.9044203758239746}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 624, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 624, 18, 13, 25968, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 498, 646, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\n3. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8780081011123169, "g_worst_claim": 0.8862170219421387, "g_content": 2.4683006773603724, "g_traj_mean": 1.9511640071868896}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 2461, 19410, 448, 8658, 350, 1885, 812, 25, 1019, 256, 481, 19219, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 55047, 264, 8413, 27292, 304, 279, 6291, 323, 10273, 279, 30717, 624, 256, 481, 57368, 325, 279, 30717, 304, 7010, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 18961, 279, 30717, 9058, 448, 264, 27292, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 2461, 1260, 30648, 350, 1885, 812, 25, 1019, 256, 481, 19219, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 20552, 279, 24937, 311, 279, 14961, 1667, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 624, 256, 481, 26530, 279, 24937, 311, 2444, 369, 264, 1393, 624, 256, 481, 57368, 325, 279, 30717, 304, 7010, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 18961, 279, 30717, 9058, 448, 264, 27292, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 16429, 27040, 25, 1019, 256, 481, 2691, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 311, 264, 19212, 624, 256, 481, 3719, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 12021, 12987, 6291, 624, 256, 481, 6771, 279, 30717, 2444, 304, 279, 6291, 311, 4057, 86480, 812, 624, 256, 481, 1416, 1667, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 11, 7500, 279, 3311, 315, 3015, 27079, 624, 256, 481, 1752, 264, 3175, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 29279, 429, 894, 18747, 32510, 525, 537, 11495, 553, 279, 12021, 12987, 382, 9485, 5413, 3410, 21700, 3602, 7373, 5510, 311, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 13, 151645], "pred": "These methods provide gentle yet effective ways to clean tarnished jewelry at home.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9642248175010572, "g_worst_claim": 0.07073003053665161, "g_content": 1.3046610905573919, "g_traj_mean": 0.8826982975006104}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.64, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "434", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to clean tarnished jewelry at home\":\n\nTo clean tarnished jewelry at home, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth in the solution. Rub the jewelry gently, then rinse it in cool water and buff it with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it with a soft, lint-free cloth. You can also use a saltwater solution by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerging the jewelry in it. Let it sit for a while, then remove and rinse it with cool water. Be sure to use a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush to clean diamonds.", "pred": "For a saltwater solution, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry. This method can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Be cautious if your jewelry has gemstones as they may not be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "For a saltwater solution, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry. This method can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Be cautious if your jewelry has gemstones as they may not be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "cot": "[\"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a mild dish soap solution for items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor. Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, rinse it in cool water, and buff it with a cloth until dry.\\n\\nFor heavier tarnish, prepare a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\nAnother option is to use warm soapy water with a soft-bristle brush or an old toothbrush. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth.\\n\\nFor a saltwater solution, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry. This method can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Be cautious if your jewelry has gemstones as they may not be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"The best way to clean tarnished jewelry at home is to use a mixture of mild dish soap and warm water to clean items that are merely dull, filmy or starting to discolor. For heavier tarnish, a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water can be used. It is also recommended to use a soft, lint-free cloth to rub and buff the jewelry until dry.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a variety of methods depending on the level of tarnish. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth.\\n\\nAnother method involves using warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat the jewelry dry with a soft cloth. If your silver is lightly tarnished, a salt bath should remove the tarnish without abrading the silver. Pour two cups of hot water into a bowl, enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\"]", "time_sec": 26.199, "generation_time_seconds": 25.1449, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0465, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 2421, 2155, 5413, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 498, 646, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 11, 14401, 279, 14961, 11, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 13, 13293, 1714, 374, 311, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 304, 279, 6291, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 11, 1281, 2704, 807, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, wet the silver, and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. Another method is to pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry in the solution. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.877648747112039, "g_worst_claim": 0.5874059200286865, "g_content": 2.5936431884765625, "g_traj_mean": 1.8465365171432495}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 23034, 12000, 26785, 6291, 369, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 13, 19219, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 279, 30717, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 73241, 432, 304, 7010, 3015, 11, 323, 11522, 432, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 2461, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 10549, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 382, 14037, 2999, 374, 311, 990, 8205, 773, 9667, 3015, 448, 264, 8413, 30117, 28300, 15625, 476, 458, 2310, 25507, 36061, 13, 7135, 279, 30717, 9058, 448, 264, 8413, 27292, 382, 2461, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 11, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 323, 1186, 19052, 279, 30717, 13, 1096, 1714, 646, 4057, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 2823, 45778, 421, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 438, 807, 1231, 537, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 13, 151645], "pred": "For a saltwater solution, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl and submerge the jewelry. This method can remove tarnish without abrading the silver. Be cautious if your jewelry has gemstones as they may not be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6931533983945706, "g_worst_claim": 0.99