{"id": "450", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression caused by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava gets emptied. This cavity creates pressure and the overground rocks collapse to create a large depression. On the other hand, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for the eruption of magma and lava. This is a depression that is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure and it typically has the opening through which lava and ash flows upwards. Craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "pred": "A caldera is a large circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, and it is used for the eruption of magma and lava. The main difference between calderas and craters is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "A caldera is a large circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, and it is used for the eruption of magma and lava. The main difference between calderas and craters is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "cot": "[\"A caldera is a large, circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.\", \"A caldera is different from a crater in terms of how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, while calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. Craters can range in size from very small to several hundred km across, while calderas are typically circular and at least one km across.\", \"A caldera is a large depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is a bowl-like structure around the opening of a volcano used for the eruption of magma and lava. Craters are formed by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across, whereas calderas are typically large (at least one kilometer across) and circular.\", \"A caldera is a large circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, and it is used for the eruption of magma and lava. The main difference between calderas and craters is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.\"]", "time_sec": 14.375, "generation_time_seconds": 13.4932, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8757, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 1393, 264, 74707, 374, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13, 151645], "pred": "A caldera is a large, circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5441651874118383, "g_worst_claim": 1.188057581583659, "g_content": 2.135120391845703, "g_traj_mean": 1.6051769256591797}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 3793, 315, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 1393, 1620, 1107, 300, 52