{"id": "452", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression made due to volcanic activity, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano that allows for the eruption of magma and lava. The primary difference between the two is the method of formation. Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large depression, while craters are formed by impacts from meteorites.", "pred": "The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. A caldera, on the other hand, is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. A caldera, on the other hand, is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.", "cot": "[\"A caldera is a large, circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.\", \"Calderas and craters are both formed by volcanic activity, but they differ in their formation processes. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed from the collapse of a magma chamber, resulting in a large, circular depression at the top of a volcano.\", \"A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.\", \"The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. A caldera, on the other hand, is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.\"]", "time_sec": 13.422, "generation_time_seconds": 12.8265, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5869, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 1393, 264, 74707, 374, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13, 151645], "pred": "A caldera is a large, circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, while a crater is caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5478274784390889, "g_worst_claim": 1.1751562754313152, "g_content": 2.115991072221236, "g_traj_mean": 1.6113393306732178}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [8851, 1107, 300, 323, 1560, 11535, 525, 2176, 14122, 553, 66064, 5702, 11, 714, 807, 1745, 304, 862, 18003, 11364, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 13289, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 504, 279, 18179, 315, 264, 63505, 24220, 11, 12942, 304, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 13, 151645], "pred": "Calderas and craters are both formed by volcanic activity, but they differ in their formation processes. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed from the collapse of a magma chamber, resulting in a large, circular depression at the top of a volcano.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7761249370333078, "g_worst_claim": 0.3964986067551833, "g_content": 1.5633668899536133, "g_traj_mean": 0.8762372732162476}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13, 151645], "pred": "A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.618371397671682, "g_worst_claim": 1.0720643361409505, "g_content": 1.5731620788574219, "g_traj_mean": 1.6080251932144165}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 1887, 6672, 1948, 1560, 11535, 323, 1620, 1107, 300, 374, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 11, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 13136, 3941, 13, 362, 1620, 97903, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 13, 151645], "pred": "The main difference between craters and calderas is how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across. A caldera, on the other hand, is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6535021286919005, "g_worst_claim": 0.993559455871582, "g_content": 1.918300986289978, "g_traj_mean": 1.470132827758789}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "460", "question": "how the colonists respond to the stamp act. why was it so upsetting?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the colonists responded to the Stamp Act of 1765 with a mix of violent and non-violent protests. They held peaceful protests, chanting slogans such as \"No Taxation without Representation,\" but these were often met with violence from British soldiers, known as Red Coats, who would come to cause fights and make arrests. The colonists also organized public protests, legislative resolutions, town meetings, and boycotts of British goods throughout the colonies. The Stamp Act was seen as particularly egregious because it was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonies, and it required the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, and other printed materials. The colonists felt that this was an overreach of British authority and a violation of their rights as British subjects. The Act was eventually repealed in 1766, but it had already sparked widespread outrage and resistance among the colonists, contributing to the growing tensions that would eventually lead to the American Revolution.", "pred": "The colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They started with peaceful protests, such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way for the British government to assert control over the colonies.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They started with peaceful protests, such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way for the British government to assert control over the colonies.", "cot": "[\"The colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They started with peaceful protests, such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way for the British government to assert control over the colonies.\", \"The colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They started with peaceful protests such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" However, some redcoats would come along and cause fights, leading to people getting arrested. This act was particularly upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonists, requiring them to use tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc.\", \"The colonists responded to the Stamp Act by engaging in both peaceful and violent protests. Peaceful actions included holding signs and chanting slogans like \\\"No Taxation without Representation.\\\" Violent reactions began when some Red Coats arrived to cause confrontations, resulting in arrests. The Stamp Act was considered so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring them to use tax-stamped paper for newspapers, legal documents, and other items. This tax was viewed as a violation of colonial rights and a means for Britain to fund its troops in the colonies without proper representation from the colonists.\", \"The colonists reacted to the Stamp Act in many ways, both violent and non-violent. One of the main reasons it was so upsetting was that it was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonists. This required them to use tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, magazines, legal documents, etc.\"]", "time_sec": 18.617, "generation_time_seconds": 17.8701, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7355, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 14889, 1671, 16441, 311, 279, 54787, 3218, 304, 5257, 5510, 11, 2176, 16401, 323, 2477, 75727, 306, 13, 2379, 3855, 448, 25650, 21907, 11, 1741, 438, 9963, 11929, 323, 76574, 77001, 1075, 330, 2753, 15190, 367, 2041, 78046, 1189, 4354, 11, 1045, 315, 279, 3731, 3539, 1862, 1035, 2525, 3156, 311, 5240, 264, 4367, 323, 633, 1251, 12517, 13, 576, 54787, 3218, 572, 773, 89969, 1576, 432, 572, 279, 1156, 2118, 3742, 25980, 389, 279, 14889, 1671, 11, 22703, 279, 990, 315, 3742, 5477, 32198, 5567, 369, 678, 31494, 11, 5777, 9293, 11, 4992, 13, 1096, 572, 3884, 438, 264, 19940, 315, 862, 3188, 323, 264, 1616, 369, 279, 7855, 3033, 311, 2060, 2524, 916, 279, 47928, 13, 151645], "pred": "The colonists responded to the Stamp Act in various ways, both violent and non-violent. They started with peaceful protests, such as holding signs and chanting slogans like \"No Taxation without Representation.\" However, some of the Red Coats would come along to cause a fight and get people arrested. The Stamp Act was so upsetting because it was the first direct tax imposed on the colonists, requiring the use of tax-stamped paper for all newspapers, legal documents, etc. This was seen as a violation of their rights and a way f