{"id": "492", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "pred": "Using a website that checks who favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Using a website that checks who favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm.", "cot": "[\"To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"Using a website that checks who favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm.\", \"You can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that checks the popular tweets which are favorited as well as people who favorited your tweets.\", \"You can use websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to see who liked your tweets.\"]", "time_sec": 9.838, "generation_time_seconds": 9.0059, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8269, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 3910, 1075, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 476, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 13037, 2138, 498, 311, 3754, 879, 374, 4694, 5853, 697, 23150, 476, 279, 1852, 23150, 438, 498, 13, 151645], "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.069036358360858, "g_worst_claim": 1.3884760992867606, "g_content": 1.9453883852277483, "g_traj_mean": 2.069962501525879}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [16429, 264, 3910, 429, 12341, 879, 6930, 23150, 11, 1741, 438, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 476, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 151645], "pred": "Using a website that checks who favorite tweets, such as Favotter.net or Favstar.fm.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 3.4241452650590376, "g_worst_claim": 3.4000979336825283, "g_content": 5.028560093470982, "g_traj_mean": 3.2547237873077393}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2610, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 264, 3910, 429, 12341, 279, 5411, 23150, 892, 525, 4694, 1608, 438, 1632, 438, 1251, 879, 4694, 1608, 697, 23150, 13, 151645], "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that checks the popular tweets which are favorited as well as people who favorited your tweets.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.1320499420166015, "g_worst_claim": 2.1320499420166015, "g_content": 2.6808885427621694, "g_traj_mean": 2.0461277961730957}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2610, 646, 990, 13037, 1741, 438, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 323, 80941, 11870, 70485, 311, 1490, 879, 14915, 697, 23150, 13, 151645], "pred": "You can use websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to see who liked your tweets.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.7358836219424294, "g_worst_claim": 2.7358836219424294, "g_content": 2.682356698172433, "g_traj_mean": 2.5953993797302246}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.86, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "493", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can tell who liked your tweet:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to track who has favorited your tweets. These websites show you a list of users who have favorited your tweets, allowing you to see who has shown interest in your content.\n\nPassage 2 provides a step-by-step guide on how to track who favorited your tweets. According to the passage, you can use a simple web-based website to check who favorited your tweets. Once you have access to this website, you can enter your tweet's URL to view a list of users who have favorited it.\n\nPassage 3 does not provide any information on tracking who favorited your tweets. However, it does mention that you can set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\n\nIn summary, to tell who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website that tracks who favorites your tweets. One such website is Favotter.net.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website that tracks who favorites your tweets. One such website is Favotter.net.", "cot": "[\"To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.\", \"To find out who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm.\", \"You can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that checks the popular tweets which are favorite as well as people who favorite your tweets.\", \"To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website that tracks who favorites your tweets. One such website is Favotter.net.\"]", "time_sec": 9.217, "generation_time_seconds": 8.5487, "selection_time_seconds": 0.6614, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [1249, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 3910, 1075, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 476, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 4220, 13037, 2138, 498, 311, 3754, 879, 374, 4694, 5853, 697, 23150, 476, 279, 1852, 23150, 438, 498, 13, 151645], "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website like Favotter.net or Favstar.fm. These websites allow you to track who is favoriting your tweets or the same tweets as you.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.1067289897373747, "g_worst_claim": 1.433115005493164, "g_content": 2.1047946384974887, "g_traj_mean": 2.093672752380371}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 1477, 700, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 11, 498, 646, 990, 13037, 1075, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 323, 80941, 11870, 70485, 13, 151645], "pred": "To find out who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.7307114260537286, "g_worst_claim": 1.574267864227295, "g_content": 3.481745719909668, "g_traj_mean": 2.86480975151062}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [2610, 646, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 553, 1667, 264, 3910, 429, 12341, 279, 5411, 23150, 892, 525, 6930, 438, 1632, 438, 1251, 879, 6930, 697, 23150, 13, 151645], "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using a website that checks the popular tweets which are favorite as well as people who favorite your tweets.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.4266815185546875, "g_worst_claim": 2.4266815185546875, "g_content": 2.987111311692458, "g_traj_mean": 2.326810121536255}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [1249, 3291, 879, 14915, 697, 11809, 11, 498, 646, 990, 264, 3910, 429, 13918, 879, 26574, 697, 23150, 13, 3776, 1741, 3910, 374, 80941, 354, 465, 5071, 13, 151645], "pred": "To tell who liked your tweet, you can use a website that tracks who favorites your tweets. One such website is Favotter.net.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.716481990915127, "g_worst_claim": 1.4486098970685686, "g_content": 1.8883039301091975, "g_traj_mean": 1.628352403640747}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 19.05, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "495", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions, also known as salary sacrifice, is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This can allow both employers and employees to save money. The salary that is waived under the salary exchange is equal to the value of the regular pension contribution made. For example, it could be 7.5% or 9% of the pay, depending on the pension scheme. The employer then pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary. Employees gain by receiving pension contributions that can be a non-cash benefit, which may be in the form of a pension payment or other financial benefits related to their retirement. For example, under the Salary Exchange program, a member of the USS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 7.5% of their pay from 1 October 2011 would have the equivalent amount paid into their pension by their employer. Similarly, a member of the USPAS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 9% of their pay from 1 August 2011 would see a corresponding amount paid into their pension by their employer.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary. Employees gain by receiving pension contributions that can be a non-cash benefit, which may be in the form of a pension payment or other financial benefits related to their retirement. For example, under the Salary Exchange program, a member of the USS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 7.5% of their pay from 1 October 2011 would have the equivalent amount paid into their pension by their employer. Similarly, a member of the USPAS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 9% of their pay from 1 August 2011 would see a corresponding amount paid into their pension by their employer.", "cot": "[\"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary. Employees gain by receiving pension contributions that can be a non-cash benefit, which may be in the form of a pension payment or other financial benefits related to their retirement. For example, under the Salary Exchange program, a member of the USS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 7.5% of their pay from 1 October 2011 would have the equivalent amount paid into their pension by their employer. Similarly, a member of the USPAS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 9% of their pay from 1 August 2011 would see a corresponding amount paid into their pension by their employer.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions are a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money. In the case of USS, for example, the regular contribution is 7.5% of the employee's pay, while for USPAS, it is 9%.\", \"Salary Exchange Pension Contributions\\nSalary Exchange, also known as Salary Sacrifice, is a method where an employee voluntarily gives up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement offers several benefits:\\n\\n1. Cost Savings: Employers benefit from lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) since they pay a reduced salary to their employees.\\n\\n2. Financial Flexibility: Employees can make pension contributions without the immediate financial burden, allowing them to save for retirement while maintaining a steady income.\\n\\n3. Employer and Employee Benefits: Both parties benefit from this arrangement. Employers save on payroll costs, and employees contribute to their retirement savings without upfront financial strain.\\n\\nSpecific Examples:\\n- For members of USS (University Superannuation Scheme), the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 was 7.5% of their pay.\\n- For members of USPAS (University Superannuation Pension Scheme), the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 was 9%.\\n\\nIn exchange, the University would pay a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This ensures that the employee's retirement savings are maintained even though their immediate take-home pay is reduced due to the salary exchange.\\n\\nThis method of pension contributions can be a strategic way for both employees and employers to save on taxes and improve personal financial security.\"]", "time_sec": 25.636, "generation_time_seconds": 24.6949, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9354, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 323, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.0620847620833316, "g_worst_claim": 0.6697822146945529, "g_content": 2.3596202305385043, "g_traj_mean": 1.6977568864822388}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 1865, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 20782, 388, 8760, 553, 12515, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 4152, 311, 279, 11046, 16107, 13, 43641, 8722, 553, 12308, 27681, 19026, 429, 646, 387, 264, 2477, 1786, 988, 8760, 11, 892, 1231, 387, 304, 279, 1352, 315, 264, 27681, 8160, 476, 1008, 5896, 7567, 5435, 311, 862, 20950, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 1212, 279, 41758, 18705, 2025, 11, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 38508, 27681, 12859, 448, 264, 5792, 18527, 315, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 1035, 614, 279, 13578, 3311, 7171, 1119, 862, 27681, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 34239, 11, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 2274, 47, 1911, 27681, 12859, 448, 264, 5792, 18527, 315, 220, 24, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 1035, 1490, 264, 12159, 3311, 7171, 1119, 862, 27681, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary. Employees gain by receiving pension contributions that can be a non-cash benefit, which may be in the form of a pension payment or other financial benefits related to their retirement. For example, under the Salary Exchange program, a member of the USS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 7.5% of their pay from 1 October 2011 would have the equivalent amount paid into their pension by their employer. Similarly, a member of the USPAS pension scheme with a regular contribution of 9% of their pay from 1 August 2011 would see a corresponding amount paid into their pension by their employer.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3376133045430572, "g_worst_claim": 0.4185878603081954, "g_content": 1.7144812222184806, "g_traj_mean": 1.1940792798995972}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 525, 264, 1714, 1380, 458, 9364, 32908, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 9677, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 758, 279, 1142, 315, 38508, 11, 369, 3110, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 279, 9364, 594, 2291, 11, 1393, 369, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 432, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions are a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both parties to save money. In the case of USS, for example, the regular contribution is 7.5% of the employee's pay, while for USPAS, it is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5889690489690849, "g_worst_claim": 0.9937106966972351, "g_content": 2.176845427482359, "g_traj_mean": 1.5143613815307617}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 18705, 80457, 96004, 198, 41673, 18705, 11, 1083, 3881, 438, 41758, 97839, 21898, 11, 374, 264, 1714, 1380, 458, 9364, 53423, 6696, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 18527, 1865, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6081, 3807, 7567, 1447, 16, 13, 11194, 57679, 25, 20782, 388, 8760, 504, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 2474, 807, 2291, 264, 11046, 16107, 311, 862, 8256, 382, 17, 13, 17503, 26141, 3147, 25, 43641, 646, 1281, 27681, 19026, 2041, 279, 13922, 5896, 22305, 11, 10693, 1105, 311, 3581, 369, 20950, 1393, 20337, 264, 24020, 7911, 382, 18, 13, 74852, 323, 16850, 38095, 25, 11733, 9677, 8760, 504, 419, 26112, 13, 20782, 388, 3581, 389, 45108, 7049, 11, 323, 8256, 16792, 311, 862, 20950, 18986, 2041, 64351, 5896, 25716, 382, 47514, 25311, 510, 12, 1752, 3613, 315, 38508, 320, 30172, 7297, 1020, 4002, 43781, 701, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 572, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 624, 12, 1752, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 320, 30172, 7297, 1020, 4002, 80457, 43781, 701, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 572, 220, 24, 34332, 641, 9289, 11, 279, 3822, 1035, 2291, 264, 12159, 3311, 1119, 279, 9760, 27681, 12859, 13, 1096, 25351, 429, 279, 9364, 594, 20950, 18986, 525, 18401, 1496, 3498, 862, 13922, 1896, 24388, 2291, 374, 11046, 4152, 311, 279, 16107, 9289, 382, 1986, 1714, 315, 27681, 19026, 646, 387, 264, 18580, 1616, 369, 2176, 8256, 323, 22426, 311, 3581, 389, 13125, 323, 7269, 4345, 5896, 4763, 13, 151645], "pred": "This method of pension contributions can be a strategic way for both employees and employers to save on taxes and improve personal financial security.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0297382655919503, "g_worst_claim": 0.014604553580284119, "g_content": 1.1168548583984375, "g_traj_mean": 0.8639512062072754}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.81, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "496", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions allow employees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement benefits both employers and employees as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees can save money by making pension contributions through this method. The amount of salary waived under the salary exchange is equivalent to the regular pension contribution, and the employer pays a corresponding amount into the pension scheme. The specific details of the contributions may vary depending on the pension scheme being used.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "cot": "[\"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of the employee's pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a system where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This method allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, in the case of USS, members contribute 7.5% of their pay, and the University pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"salary exchange pension contributions are a way where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer.\"]", "time_sec": 14.873, "generation_time_seconds": 14.1079, "selection_time_seconds": 0.754, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 323, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.0445073201256108, "g_worst_claim": 0.6607810656229655, "g_content": 2.3815707252139138, "g_traj_mean": 1.6736624240875244}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 1865, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 279, 9364, 594, 2291, 11, 323, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 24, 14360, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of the employee's pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.144490533893944, "g_worst_claim": 0.6996716923183866, "g_content": 2.363548066880968, "g_traj_mean": 1.5959978103637695}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 8300, 311, 264, 1849, 1380, 458, 9364, 32908, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 1865, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 1714, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 304, 279, 1142, 315, 38508, 11, 3613, 16792, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 862, 2291, 11, 323, 279, 3822, 21241, 264, 12159, 3311, 1119, 279, 9760, 27681, 12859, 13, 151645], "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a system where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This method allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, in the case of USS, members contribute 7.5% of their pay, and the University pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7038695780454727, "g_worst_claim": 0.7641705671946207, "g_content": 2.087944231535259, "g_traj_mean": 1.4466454982757568}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [51610, 9289, 27681, 19026, 525, 264, 1616, 1380, 458, 9364, 32908, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 151645], "pred": "salary exchange pension contributions are a way where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5609956556750881, "g_worst_claim": 1.5609956556750881, "g_content": 2.035922050476074, "g_traj_mean": 1.4830315113067627}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "497", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.", "cot": "[\"Salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of their pay, and for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Answer: Salary exchange pension contributions are a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. Employers benefit from this arrangement as they pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) by paying their employees a reduced salary. The employee receives a pension contribution as compensation for waiving part of their salary.\\n\\nFor example, under the Salary Exchange scheme, members of USS have a regular pension contribution of 7.5% of their pay, while members of USPAS have a regular contribution of 9%. In exchange, the University would pay a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions are a way for both employers and employees to save money. Under this arrangement, employees agree to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This can lead to employers paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. The passage also mentions that salary exchange offers a way to set up pension payments that can allow both employers and their employees to make a saving.\", \"salary exchange pension contributions refer to a situation where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment that is paid by their employer.\"]", "time_sec": 16.762, "generation_time_seconds": 15.7733, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9805, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 448, 2456, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 374, 220, 22, 