{"id": "521", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "pred": "An entry ticket for a raffle can have a number from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets can be worth $50 each. If a table consists of 20 individuals, it constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. There are two $500 prizes in the raffle, one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. The 50-50 draw is held during a single day at a particular event and the gross proceeds of the 50-50 tickets sold go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw which occurs towards the end of the event. The remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "An entry ticket for a raffle can have a number from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets can be worth $50 each. If a table consists of 20 individuals, it constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. There are two $500 prizes in the raffle, one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled. The 50-50 draw is held during a single day at a particular event and 