 electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. When it works as a switch, it can control the flow of electricity by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base. Transistors are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. It is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications. Unlike the field-effect transistor, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.424047899694609, "g_worst_claim": -0.04616241369928632, "g_content": 2.0886250108775526, "g_traj_mean": 1.4801775217056274}}], "selected_rollout": 3, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.36, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "551", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how a transistor works:\n\n1. A transistor functions as both an amplifier and a switch.\n2. The transistor's design allows it to control a larger supply of electricity with a small amount of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\n3. The base of the transistor pulls electrons from the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\n4. The transistor can take in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produce a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current) when it works as an amplifier.\n5. The holes in the base of the transistor act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is in its off state.\n6. When the electrons and holes move across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor begins to work.\n7. The collector current is approximately beta (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, and it is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications.\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages.", "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch. This is accomplished by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than does the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch. This is accomplished by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than does the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.", "cot": "[\"A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch. This is accomplished by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than does the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.\", \"A transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. For instance, in the case of an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. The movement of electrons in a transistor is controlled by the base, which pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than the emitter. This movement creates a flow of electricity through the transistor, passing from the emitter to the collector.\", \"A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit, and it can function as an amplifier or a switch.\\n\\nWhen a transistor works as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produces a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current). \\n\\nThe design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. For example, the base of a transistor pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\\n\\nWhen the transistor works as a switch, the holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. However, when the electrons and holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor works as a switch, allowing current to flow from the emitter to the collector.\\n\\nIn summary, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can perform two different jobs: amplifying or switching electronic signals and electrical power. It serves as an amplifier when it increases the strength of an input signal and as a switch when it allows or blocks the flow of current.\", \"A transistor is a semiconductor device that serves as both an amplifier and a switch. It functions by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a larger supply of electricity, akin to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When functions as an amplifier, it accepts a small electric current (input current) at one end and produces a much larger electric current (output current) at the other end. Transistors can also act as switches when the electrons and holes in the base allow current to flow from the emitter to the collector. They are composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit and can have a common-emitter current gain of approximately β, which is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors.\"]", "time_sec": 28.966, "generation_time_seconds": 28.0116, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9453, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [32, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 576, 2884, 315, 264, 97941, 6147, 432, 311, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1096, 374, 26237, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 1753, 1075, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 576, 2331, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 1576, 432, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 1558, 279, 47263, 13, 1096, 7203, 315, 56578, 11450, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 13, 576, 1482, 16211, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1526, 279, 2331, 13, 151645], "pred": "A transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. The design of a transistor allows it to function as an amplifier or a switch. This is accomplished by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. The base pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than does the emitter. This movement of electrons creates a flow of electricity through the transistor. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.525866023859824, "g_worst_claim": 0.5543708801269531, "g_content": 1.8917312622070312, "g_traj_mean": 1.5610300302505493}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [32, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 2987, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 1753, 1075, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 1752, 2867, 11, 304, 279, 1142, 315, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 576, 7203, 315, 56578, 304, 264, 97941, 374, 14071, 553, 279, 2331, 11, 892, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 1576, 432, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 279, 47263, 13, 1096, 7203, 11450, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 11, 12299, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 13, 151645], "pred": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as either an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water. For instance, in the case of an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. The movement of electrons in a transistor is controlled by the base, which pulls electrons from the emitter because it has a more positive voltage than the emitter. This movement creates a flow of electricity through the transistor, passing from the emitter to the collector.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3656888079436489, "g_worst_claim": 0.30022549629211426, "g_content": 2.035371326264881, "g_traj_mean": 1.4793250560760498}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [32, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 11, 323, 432, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 382, 4498, 264, 97941, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 1782, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 320, 1782, 2550, 1482, 568, 4710, 785, 2884, 315, 264, 97941, 6147, 432, 311, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 279, 2331, 315, 264, 97941, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 1576, 432, 702, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 1091, 279, 47263, 11, 6825, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 382, 4498, 279, 97941, 4278, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 438, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 4354, 11, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 11, 279, 97941, 4278, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 10693, 1482, 311, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 382, 641, 12126, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 2736, 1378, 2155, 6887, 25, 22395, 7766, 476, 27765, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 17045, 438, 458, 60066, 979, 432, 12703, 279, 8170, 315, 458, 1946, 8286, 323, 438, 264, 3398, 979, 4