, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 374, 10660, 553, 279, 24394, 30956, 65457, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 11, 1393, 35324, 82, 3881, 438, 43215, 525, 8480, 369, 8286, 18368, 304, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 624, 18, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 19259, 11, 448, 7761, 82316, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 315, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 59780, 33213, 311, 29282, 17289, 11, 19853, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 374, 1483, 553, 279, 3972, 77638, 739, 311, 29282, 17289, 13, 151645], "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\n2. Transmission is performed by the electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, while Chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, whereas the circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.7544150606185152, "g_worst_claim": 1.1961694955825806, "g_content": 2.2090221586681547, "g_traj_mean": 1.4463614225387573}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 835, 77638, 1849, 16792, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 5089, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 23415, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 374, 10660, 553, 24394, 30956, 65457, 11, 19853, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 5711, 43215, 438, 11483, 17289, 624, 18, 13, 758, 279, 22596, 1849, 11, 7761, 525, 82316, 30878, 11, 19853, 279, 35753, 304, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 525, 43179, 323, 537, 21893, 8433, 624, 19, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 1356, 44703, 17289, 1667, 33213, 11, 304, 12872, 311, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 892, 5711, 279, 4225, 37120, 1849, 311, 29282, 17289, 13, 151645], "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas the endocrine system uses hormones as chemical signals.\n3. In the nervous system, cells are interconnected continuously, whereas the organs in the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system transmits signals using neurons, in contrast to the endocrine system which uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.247355933017609, "g_worst_claim": 0.24360287189483643, "g_content": 1.647364404466417, "g_traj_mean": 1.0333017110824585}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "636", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here are the key differences and similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\n* Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, it is done through chemicals called hormones.\n* The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system.\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n* Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs.\n\nTherefore, based on the given passages, the nervous system and endocrine system have different structures and methods of transmission, but they share similar functions and regulatory mechanisms.", "pred": "Differences:\n- The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made up of glands.\n- Transmission is performed by electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\n- The nervous system is continuous, with interconnected cells, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\n- The circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals, while the nervous system utilizes neurons for signaling.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Differences:\n- The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made up of glands.\n- Transmission is performed by electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\n- The nervous system is continuous, with interconnected cells, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\n- The circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals, while the nervous system utilizes neurons for signaling.", "cot": "[\"Similarities:\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the Endocrine System, it is done by hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Similarities:\\n- Both systems maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating activities.\\n- Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n- The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by hormones.\\n- The nervous system is continuous, with interconnected cells, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs.\\n- The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Similarities:\\n- Both systems contribute to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating activities.\\n- Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n- The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made up of glands.\\n- Transmission is performed by electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\\n- The nervous system is continuous, with interconnected cells, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\\n- The circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals, while the nervous system utilizes neurons for signaling.\", \"Similarities:\\n1. Both the nervous system and endocrine system are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete.\\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\"]", "time_sec": 23.832, "generation_time_seconds": 23.2461, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5769, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 1