{"id": "664", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "When you breathe in, or inhale, the intercostal muscles contract and expand the ribcage while the diaphragm contracts and pulls downward to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest and allows air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe. When you breathe out, or exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax. This reduces the space in the chest cavity and forces out air that is rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "pred": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth. Breathing out requires no effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity. When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This causes the pressure inside the chest to lower, and air is sucked into the lungs.\", \"When you breathe in (inhale), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 22.735, "generation_time_seconds": 22.1851, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5453, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0590580113981136, "g_worst_claim": 0.020858082506391738, "g_content": 1.785816566616881, "g_traj_mean": 1.1707022190093994}