 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.090549034999792, "g_worst_claim": -0.2507615089416504, "g_content": 1.865094502766927, "g_traj_mean": 1.1863298416137695}}], "selected_rollout": 1, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 80.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "665", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This forces air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.", "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe out, or exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth. Breathing out requires no effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.\", \"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This causes pressure inside the chest to drop, and air is sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This makes the space in the chest cavity smaller, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"Answer: When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 20.428, "generation_time_seconds": 19.9011, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5198, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0694723590922655, "g_worst_claim": 0.0031363736424181196, "g_content": 1.7863278856464462, "g_traj_mean": 1.1873588562011719}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 476, 56659, 1574, 11, 697, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 311,