{"id": "667", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the answers to the question \"What happens when you breathe in and out?\":\n\nWhen you breathe in (inspiration):\n\n* The intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage.\n* The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\n* Pressure inside the chest is lowered and air is sucked into the lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation):\n\n* Your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n* The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity.\n* Air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\n\nTherefore, when you breathe in, the lungs take in oxygen-rich air, and when you breathe out, the lungs expel carbon dioxide-rich air.", "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\\n\\nAnswer: During inhalation, the intercostal muscles and diaphragm contract, increasing the volume of the chest and drawing air into the lungs. During exhalation, these muscles relax, reducing the chest volume and forcing air out of the lungs.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 22.388, "generation_time_seconds": 21.6007, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7807, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0600918470196936, "g_worst_claim": 0.017891057663493685, "g_content": 1.7929886462641698, "g_traj_mean": 1.1773903369903564}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 382, 16141, 25, 11954, 76673, 367, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 323, 1853, 94796, 76, 5116, 11, 7703, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 323, 13330, 3720, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 11954, 505, 11866, 367, 11, 1493, 23648, 11967, 11, 17719, 279, 15138, 8123, 323, 24913, 3720, 700, 315, 279, 44174, 13, 151645], "pred": "During inhalation, the intercostal muscles and diaphragm contract, increasing the volume of the chest and drawing air into the lungs. During exhalation, these muscles relax, reducing the chest volume and forcing air out of the lungs.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9223074883545749, "g_worst_claim": -0.12113051861524582, "g_content": 1.3699188232421875, "g_traj_mean": 0.9631140232086182}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1535416307215394, "g_worst_claim": 0.22559678554534912, "g_content": 1.961640731148098, "g_traj_mean": 1.2221851348876953}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 11, 17719, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.338198309127616, "g_worst_claim": 0.7417382664150662, "g_content": 1.9290513019172513, "g_traj_mean": 1.3546679019927979}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 3, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 74.67, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "668", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is what happens when you breathe in and out:\n\nWhen you breathe in (inhalation):\n\n* The intercostal muscles contract, causing the ribcage to expand.\n* The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\n* Pressure inside the chest is lowered, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation):\n\n* The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n* The intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity.\n* Air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nSo, in summary, when you breathe in, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract to bring air into the lungs, and when you breathe out, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax to force air out of the lungs.", "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in (inhalation), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. Pressure inside the chest decreases, and air is drawn into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, which causes air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. Simultaneously, the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest cavity. This action lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax to bring the ribcage back to its original position. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 21.366, "generation_time_seconds": 20.5272, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8339, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0605454539436667, "g_worst_claim": 0.0086462688114908, "g_content": 1.7780811085420496, "g_traj_mean": 1.181026577949524}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 38990, 4766, 279, 15138, 42054, 11, 323, 3720, 374, 14764, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 24913, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 700, 315, 279, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. Pressure inside the chest decreases, and air is drawn into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, forcing air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9636985331558734, "g_worst_claim": 0.04676932096481323, "g_content": 1.5491378090598367, "g_traj_mean": 1.0973289012908936}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 1083, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 892, 11137, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, which causes air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1333491152165225, "g_worst_claim": 0.08925286361149379, "g_content": 1.793200225830078, "g_traj_mean": 1.1893532276153564}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 1330, 28479, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 4467, 494, 20315, 11, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1096, 1917, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 382, 4498, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 63643, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 17719, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1083, 11967, 311, 4446, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 1182, 311, 1181, 4024, 2309, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe out (expiration), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles also relax to bring the ribcage back to its original position. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.9921637109903582, "g_worst_claim": 0.028270010437284197, "g_content": 1.3624340057373048, "g_traj_mean": 0.9766807556152344}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 69.28, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "669", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the given passages:\n\nWhen you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to enter the lungs. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract, expanding the ribcage and further increasing the space in the chest cavity. Air enters the lungs through the windpipe (trachea) and fills the expanded lung tissue.\n\nWhen you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, allowing the chest cavity to decrease in size. As the space in the chest cavity decreases, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nTherefore, when you breathe in and out, air enters the lungs during inhalation and leaves the lungs during exhalation.", "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and pressure inside the chest is lowered, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and pressure inside the chest is lowered, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, causing a decrease in pressure inside the chest, which pulls air into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the intercostal muscles relax and move upward, reducing the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, causing the volume of the chest to decrease, forcing air out of the lungs.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and pressure inside the chest is lowered, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage and allowing the diaphragm to contract, pulling downwards and increasing the volume of the chest, resulting in the pressure inside the chest being lowered. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. Conversely, when you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 21.063, "generation_time_seconds": 20.0755, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9827, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 258, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 327, 11866, 367, 701, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out (exhalation), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.053506192289017, "g_worst_claim": 0.016138033734427557, "g_content": 1.7603668512082566, "g_traj_mean": 1.1628522872924805}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 1330, 28479, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 264, 18472, 304, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 892, 33045, 3720, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 320, 63643, 701, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 323, 3271, 39772, 11, 17719, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 11, 14381, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 311, 18472, 11, 24913, 3720, 700, 315, 279, 44174, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in (inspiration), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, causing a decrease in pressure inside the chest, which pulls air into the lungs. When you breathe out (expiration), the intercostal muscles relax and move upward, reducing the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, causing the volume of the chest to decrease, forcing air out of the lungs.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 0.7688348159576074, "g_worst_claim": -0.07282076563153948, "g_content": 1.1922208612615413, "g_traj_mean": 0.7740648984909058}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 576, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 11, 323, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 374, 38820, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 3197, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest, and pressure inside the chest is lowered, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity, and the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.3036473981573815, "g_worst_claim": 0.7329970465766059, "g_content": 1.8988839721679687, "g_traj_mean": 1.2937055826187134}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 323, 10693, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 311, 5116, 11, 22266, 91270, 323, 7703, 279, 8123, 315, 279, 15138, 11, 12942, 304, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 1660, 38820, 13, 1096, 72015, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 52212, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 81571, 11, 979, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 279, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 11, 323, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1083, 11967, 311, 7949, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 323, 1221, 700, 315, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "pred": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage and allowing the diaphragm to contract, pulling downwards and increasing the volume of the chest, resulting in the pressure inside the chest being lowered. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. Conversely, when you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.0639360353463212, "g_worst_claim": 0.6477121777004666, "g_content": 1.755244071023506, "g_traj_mean": 1.0851095914840698}}], "selected_rollout": 2, "random_sel_rollout": 2, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.49, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "670", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each item of inventory when it is sold. This is calculated by taking the average cost of all inventories currently available. This cost fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average cost per unit is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This cost is then assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is often used when it's impossible to assign specific costs to individual units or when the accounting system isn't sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using this method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"The weighted average method of costing inventory is a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale (which includes the beginning inventory and net purchases) by the number of units available for sale. This weighted-average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The net result is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"When a unit of inventory is sold using the weighted average method, the average cost of all inventories currently available is assigned to the item sold. This average price is determined by dividing the total cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. The net result of using weighted average costing is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled, making it impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\"]", "time_sec": 19.725, "generation_time_seconds": 18.7733, "selection_time_seconds": 0.9454, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8914374800325202, "g_worst_claim": 1.1701359748840332, "g_content": 2.396865246342678, "g_traj_mean": 1.7047511339187622}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 53724, 15444, 374, 264, 1714, 429, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 5461, 2783, 374, 16588, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 320, 8206, 5646, 279, 7167, 15444, 323, 4179, 23491, 8, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 13, 1096, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 374, 1221, 1483, 311, 9793, 264, 2783, 311, 2176, 279, 13391, 15444, 323, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 6088, 13, 576, 4179, 1102, 374, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of costing inventory is a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale (which includes the beginning inventory and net purchases) by the number of units available for sale. This weighted-average cost per unit is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The net result is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.5057625743175487, "g_worst_claim": 0.5881728547992129, "g_content": 1.9266674487621753, "g_traj_mean": 1.424501895904541}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [4498, 264, 4982, 315, 15444, 374, 6088, 1667, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 11, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 374, 12607, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 1096, 5461, 3349, 374, 10838, 553, 49702, 279, 2790, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 11, 892, 35408, 279, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 13, 576, 4179, 1102, 315, 1667, 36824, 5461, 53724, 374, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 151645], "pred": "When a unit of inventory is sold using the weighted average method, the average cost of all inventories currently available is assigned to the item sold. This average price is determined by dividing the total cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. The net result of using weighted average costing is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6600621146606223, "g_worst_claim": 0.5468637943267822, "g_content": 2.254622365914139, "g_traj_mean": 1.6734641790390015}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 5461, 2783, 374, 16588, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 13, 576, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 374, 279, 2629, 315, 7167, 15444, 323, 4179, 23491, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 946, 5311, 832, 11, 3259, 432, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979,