{"id": "672", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it can be understood that the Weighted Average Costing (WAC) method is used to determine the average cost of inventory when a unit is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and uses it to calculate the weighted average cost per unit. WAC is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and it's difficult to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available, and this average price is then assigned to the item sold. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available, and this average price is then assigned to the item sold. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Answer: The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available, and this average price is then assigned to the item sold. This method is commonly used when inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, yielding the weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale includes the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available. This average price is then assigned to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\"]", "time_sec": 20.322, "generation_time_seconds": 19.2487, "selection_time_seconds": 1.0677, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.9511664262350363, "g_worst_claim": 1.1727675199508667, "g_content": 2.465757033404182, "g_traj_mean": 1.7302281856536865}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [16141, 25, 576, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458