868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, yielding the weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale includes the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.6440827612782438, "g_worst_claim": 0.6534391486126444, "g_content": 2.0646080440945096, "g_traj_mean": 1.5925410985946655}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 3197, 264, 4982, 315, 15444, 374, 6088, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 13, 1096, 5461, 3349, 374, 1221, 12607, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available. This average price is then assigned to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8273629341321769, "g_worst_claim": 1.13719642162323, "g_content": 2.388718440614898, "g_traj_mean": 1.7247234582901}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 1, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 71.3, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "673", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer to the question:\n\nThe weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns that average price to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost per unit, divide the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The weighted-average figure can then be used to assign costs to both ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using the weighted average costing method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nWeighted average costing is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method calculates the average cost by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. It yields a weighted-average cost per unit, which is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average costing method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"Inventory cost is calculated based on the average cost of all available inventories when a unit is sold.\", \"The weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold, based on the average cost of all inventories currently available. This method updates the average cost when inventory prices fluctuate, ensuring that the recorded inventory value reflects a value between the oldest and newest units purchased. It is commonly used in situations where inventory items are too intermingled to assign a specific cost to individual units or where the accounting system cannot track FIFO (First-In, First-Out) or LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) inventory layers.\"]", "time_sec": 17.196, "generation_time_seconds": 16.3601, "selection_time_seconds": 0.8294, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.956175890024986, "g_worst_claim": 1.1434334516525269, "g_content": 2.4663277420343137, "g_traj_mean": 1.7321016788482666}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2