{"id": "675", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to your question:\n\nThe weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale to calculate the weighted-average cost per unit. This figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and cannot be assigned a specific cost, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "cot": "[\"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale to determine the weighted-average cost per unit. This weighted-average figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The net result of using the weighted average costing method is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or where the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"inventory costing weighted average\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where it is difficult to assign specific costs to individual units of inventory or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This method helps to smooth out inventory costs, as it takes into account the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold.\"]", "time_sec": 18.469, "generation_time_seconds": 17.7233, "selection_time_seconds": 0.7411, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.8693776611471844, "g_worst_claim": 1.2096631526947021, "g_content": 2.3815644208122704, "g_traj_mean": 1.6982182264328003}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 17601, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 311, 8253, 279, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 13, 1096, 36824, 77426, 7071, 374, 1221, 1483, 311, 9793, 264, 2783, 311, 2176, 13391, 15444, 323, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 6088, 13, 576, 4179, 1102, 315, 1667, 279, 36824, 5461, 53724, 1714, 374, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 1380, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method involves dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale to determine the weighted-average cost per unit. This weighted-average figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The net result of using the weighted average costing method is that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or where the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.4834669229135695, "g_worst_claim": 0.7001841408865792, "g_content": 1.9374366173377404, "g_traj_mean": 1.4675610065460205}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [31093, 53724, 36824, 5461, 151645], "pred": "inventory costing weighted average", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.661015510559082, "g_worst_claim": 1.661015510559082, "g_content": 1.661015510559082, "g_traj_mean": 1.263702154159546}}, {"rollout": 3, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [785, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 5461, 2783, 374, 10838, 553, 49702, 279, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 8153, 2500, 369, 6278, 11, 892, 35408, 279, 36824, 77426, 2783, 817, 4982, 13, 576, 2783, 315, 11561, 2500, 369, 6278, 374, 279, 2629, 315, 7167, 15444, 323, 4179, 23491, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 432, 374, 5000, 311, 9793, 3151, 7049, 311, 3842, 8153, 315, 15444, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 26779, 3322, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 1096, 1714, 8609, 311, 10876, 700, 15444, 7049, 11, 438, 432, 4990, 1119, 2692, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 979, 264, 4982, 374, 6088, 13, 151645], "pred": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, which yields the weighted-average cost per unit. The cost of goods available for sale is the sum of beginning inventory and net purchases. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where it is difficult to assign specific costs to individual units of inventory or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. This method helps to smooth out inventory costs, as it takes into account the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit is sold.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 1.1046693452783902, "g_worst_claim": 0.4672244389851888, "g_content": 1.4433886384310788, "g_traj_mean": 1.1006330251693726}}], "selected_rollout": 0, "random_sel_rollout": 0, "rank_score": "g_content", "partner": "psg-borrow", "error": null, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 60.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "677", "question": "benefits and side effects of drinking clove tea", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the benefits of drinking clove tea include soothing the senses, relieving stress, providing relief from abdominal pain and aiding digestion. The side effects of drinking clove tea are not mentioned in the given passages.", "pred": "Side effects:\nThe provided passage does not mention any side effects of drinking clove tea.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "bon_grounding_random_sel", "raw_output": "Side effects:\nThe provided passage does not mention any side effects of drinking clove tea.", "cot": "[\"Benefits of drinking clove tea include soothing the senses, relieving stress, and providing relief from abdominal pain, gas pain, and other stomach upsets. It also stimulates blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions to aid in digestion. Clove tea compresses can be used to relieve pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries, and it can be applied to unbroken skin. If there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury, then ice-cold tea compresses should be used. Warm tea works better with arthritic pain.\", \"Benefits:\\n1. Soothes the senses and relieves stress.\\n2. Provides relief from stress when mixed with other herbs (basil, mint, cardamom) and honey.\\n3. Offers skin benefits.\\n4. Relieves pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries due to its analgesic effects.\\n5. Serves as an excellent way to relieve pain from any injury when applied to unbroken skin.\\n6. Used to soothe abdominal pain for centuries.\\n7. Helps with gas pain or other stomach upsets.\\n8. Stimulates blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions to aid in digestion.\\n\\nSide effects are not mentioned in the provided passages.\", \"Benefits:\\n1. Soothes the senses and relieves stress in the body.\\n2. Can be mixed with basil, mint, and cardamom to make a flavoured tea.\\n3. Provides relief from stress when taken with honey.\\n4. Has skin benefits.\\n5. Can relieve pain from any muscle, joint, or ligament injury.\\n6. Stimulates blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions.\\n7. Useful for abdominal pain, gas pain, and other stomach upsets.\\n\\nSide effects:\\nThe provided passage does not mention any side effects of drinking clove tea.\", \"Benefits of drinking clove tea include:\\n- Soothing the senses and relieving stress in the body\\n- Providing relief from stress when mixed with basil, mint, and cardamom in water and taken with honey\\n- Having skin benefits\\n- Relieving pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries through analgesic effects\\n- Being applied to unbroken skin for pain relief\\n- Working better for arthritic pain when warm\\n- Soothing abdominal pain and aiding in digestion\\n\\nThere are no mentioned side effects in the provided passages.\"]", "time_sec": 20.269, "generation_time_seconds": 19.6964, "selection_time_seconds": 0.5642, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "sampled": false, "token_ids": [84323, 315, 16163, 5292, 586, 15243, 2924, 64364, 279, 37107, 11, 58544, 4405, 8464, 11, 323, 8241, 15957, 504, 55956, 6646, 11, 6819, 6646, 11, 323, 1008, 22350, 705, 4917, 13, 1084, 1083, 94355, 6543, 6396, 11, 84557, 5670, 11, 323, 88285, 6234, 908, 311, 12296, 304, 73402, 13, 18679, 586, 15243, 24611, 288, 646, 387, 1483, 311, 47739, 6646, 504, 15747, 11, 10284, 11, 476, 28313, 2838, 14970, 11, 323, 432, 646, 387, 9251, 311, 650, 48909, 6787, 13, 1416, 1052, 374, 264, 2696, 315, 54207, 10342, 448, 279, 10895, 11, 1221, 9853, 1786, 813, 15243, 24611, 288, 1265, 387, 1483, 13, 45763, 15243, 4278, 2664, 448, 796, 339, 49208, 6646, 13, 151645], "pred": "Benefits of drinking clove tea include soothing the senses, relieving stress, and providing relief from abdominal pain, gas pain, and other stomach upsets. It also stimulates blood flow, saliva production, and gastric secretions to aid in digestion. Clove tea compresses can be used to relieve pain from muscle, joint, or ligament injuries, and it can be applied to unbroken skin. If there is a lot of swelling noted with the injury, then ice-cold tea compresses should be used. Warm tea works better with arthritic pain.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.179735530040706, "g_worst_claim": 1.0638908386230468, "g_content": 3.1081018621271306, "g_traj_mean": 2.0923030376434326}}, {"rollout": 1, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [84323, 510, 16, 13, 2055, 78, 6375, 279, 37107, 323, 1351, 38135, 8464, 624, 17, 13, 36617, 15957, 504, 8464, 979, 9519, 448, 1008, 41293, 320, 17797, 321, 11, 28337, 11, 3701, 309, 316, 8, 323, 25744, 624, 18, 13, 51318, 6787, 7567, 624, 19, 13, 6512, 38135, 6646, 504, 15747, 11, 10284, 11, 476, 28313, 2838, 14970, 4152, 311, 1181, 3181, 4188, 292, 6239, 624, 20, 13, 328, 13280, 438, 458, 9073, 1616, 311, 47739, 6646, 504, 894, 10895, 979, 9251, 311, 650, 48909, 6787, 624, 21, 13, 12199, 311, 43176, 1782, 55956, 6646, 369, 23631, 624, 22, 13, 88516, 448, 6819, 6646, 476, 1008, 22350, 705, 4917, 624, 23, 13, 70772, 23156, 6543, 6396, 11, 84557, 5670, 11, 323, 88285, 6234, 908, 311, 12296, 304, 73402, 382, 16384, 6239, 525, 537, 9733, 304, 279, 3897, 46769, 13, 151645], "pred": "Side effects are not mentioned in the provided passages.", "grounding_scores": {"g_mean_claim": 2.4371892396066057, "g_worst_claim": 0.5536685943603515, "g_content": 2.7500038146972656, "g_traj_mean": 1.8724267482757568}}, {"rollout": 2, "sampled": true, "token_ids": [84323, 510, 16, 13, 2055, 78, 6375, 279, 37107, 323, 1351, 38135, 8464, 304, 279, 2487, 624, 17, 13, 2980, 387, 9519, 448, 67086, 11, 28337, 11, 323, 3701, 309, 316, 311, 1281, 264, 18276, 20394, 15243, 624, 18, 13, 36617, 15957, 504, 8464, 979, 4429, 448, 25744, 624, 19, 13, 11443, 6787, 7567, 624, 20, 13, 2980, 47739, 6646, 504, 894, 15747, 11, 10284, 11, 476, 28313, 2838, 10895, 624, 21, 13, 70772, 23156, 6543, 6396, 11, 84557, 5670, 11, 323, 88285, 6234, 908, 624, 22, 13,